WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:04.340 --> 00:00:06.410
- Welcome, everybody.

00:00:06.410 --> 00:00:10.203
Thank you for joining us
today for a hybrid seminar

00:00:10.203 --> 00:00:12.519
sponsored by Program on
Refugees, Forced Displacement,

00:00:12.519 --> 00:00:14.520
and Humanitarian Responses
at the MacMillan.

00:00:14.520 --> 00:00:16.919
My name is Catherine Panter-Brick.

00:00:16.919 --> 00:00:19.924
I am Professor of Anthropology,
Health and Global Affairs

00:00:19.924 --> 00:00:22.110
at Yale, and today I have
the pleasure of introducing

00:00:22.110 --> 00:00:24.382
and hosting our speaker.

00:00:24.382 --> 00:00:28.473
Let me introduce you briefly
to professor Michael Pluess.

00:00:29.860 --> 00:00:33.680
He is Professor in
Developmental Psychology

00:00:34.543 --> 00:00:36.414
at Queen Mary University of London,

00:00:36.414 --> 00:00:38.210
so obviously joins us here from the UK

00:00:38.210 --> 00:00:39.653
for our hybrid seminar.

00:00:40.725 --> 00:00:41.690
He came to the field of psychology

00:00:41.690 --> 00:00:43.600
after training in chemistry, music,

00:00:43.600 --> 00:00:46.560
and therefore has a very
interesting personal

00:00:47.401 --> 00:00:49.743
and scientific background
to offer us today.

00:00:50.717 --> 00:00:53.480
He's very well known
internationally for his work

00:00:53.480 --> 00:00:55.980
on environmental sensitivity,

00:00:55.980 --> 00:00:57.890
which anchors in science a notion

00:00:58.802 --> 00:01:00.321
that some people are more effected

00:01:00.321 --> 00:01:02.400
by the very same experience,
for example, stress

00:01:02.400 --> 00:01:04.290
than others because they're more sensitive

00:01:04.290 --> 00:01:06.150
to environmental conditions,

00:01:06.150 --> 00:01:09.113
and that sensitivity shapes
our human development

00:01:09.113 --> 00:01:12.900
over the life course,
as he shows in his work.

00:01:12.900 --> 00:01:14.830
He's also known for work

00:01:14.830 --> 00:01:17.003
on positive development and resilience,

00:01:17.919 --> 00:01:20.870
evaluating interventions
aimed to promote well-being

00:01:20.870 --> 00:01:23.670
in children and adults,
and he leads research

00:01:23.670 --> 00:01:26.500
on mental health in humanitarian crisis,

00:01:26.500 --> 00:01:29.360
which is where in the area
in which our work intersects

00:01:29.360 --> 00:01:31.170
and how I came to know him

00:01:31.170 --> 00:01:35.539
as a very generous,
highly productive scholar,

00:01:35.539 --> 00:01:36.960
interested in interdisciplinary work

00:01:36.960 --> 00:01:39.840
and transnational collaborations.

00:01:39.840 --> 00:01:43.801
So it's really my pleasure to
introduce him to you today.

00:01:43.801 --> 00:01:45.200
He'll talk about the
research that he has led

00:01:45.200 --> 00:01:47.970
on mental health of
Syrian refugee children,

00:01:47.970 --> 00:01:50.550
living in precarious
conditions in Lebanon,

00:01:50.550 --> 00:01:55.550
and the work he has led on
phone-based psychological therapy

00:01:55.960 --> 00:01:56.983
in Lebanon.

00:01:57.900 --> 00:01:59.710
The title of this is, you know,

00:01:59.710 --> 00:02:02.975
it's really a great
pleasure to welcome him.

00:02:02.975 --> 00:02:04.382
The title of his talk today is

00:02:04.382 --> 00:02:05.807
"Prevalence, Predictors and Treatment

00:02:05.807 --> 00:02:09.770
"of Mental Health Problems
in Syrian Refugee Children."

00:02:09.770 --> 00:02:12.000
Before we start, please know

00:02:12.000 --> 00:02:15.520
that Dr. Pluess welcomes
questions, clarifications

00:02:15.520 --> 00:02:18.082
or concerns during his presentation.

00:02:18.082 --> 00:02:20.420
So if you have any, please
put that in the chat

00:02:20.420 --> 00:02:23.760
or raise your hand, and
we'll be able to nudge him

00:02:23.760 --> 00:02:28.760
to stop and respond to
you as he talks to us.

00:02:28.870 --> 00:02:31.080
So welcome, Michael,

00:02:31.080 --> 00:02:33.842
and it's a great pleasure
to hand off to you.

00:02:33.842 --> 00:02:35.690
Thank you for joining us today.

00:02:35.690 --> 00:02:38.150
- Well, thank you very much
for the kind introduction,

00:02:38.150 --> 00:02:40.590
and it's really great to be here.

00:02:40.590 --> 00:02:42.910
I've met Catherine quite a while ago

00:02:42.910 --> 00:02:46.520
when I was at the very beginning
of developing this idea.

00:02:46.520 --> 00:02:49.445
So it's fantastic to be able to be here

00:02:49.445 --> 00:02:50.760
and to share where we've been,

00:02:50.760 --> 00:02:52.920
and what we've managed to do

00:02:52.920 --> 00:02:54.934
over the last few years.

00:02:54.934 --> 00:02:56.100
So I wish I was there in person.

00:02:56.100 --> 00:03:01.100
I was, in my music days
actually, I was at Yale once,

00:03:02.830 --> 00:03:05.010
but, and I remember it was,

00:03:05.010 --> 00:03:07.060
it's really a wonderful, beautiful place.

00:03:08.276 --> 00:03:10.890
So let me start with giving an overview

00:03:10.890 --> 00:03:13.730
for what I'd like to cover today.

00:03:13.730 --> 00:03:15.590
So there's quite a few things.

00:03:15.590 --> 00:03:20.234
I'd like to first give a
background, the Syrian context,

00:03:20.234 --> 00:03:21.440
and then I'll introduce the BIOPATH study

00:03:21.440 --> 00:03:22.743
and several findings.

00:03:23.592 --> 00:03:26.056
So I will talk about the
prevalence and predictors

00:03:26.056 --> 00:03:27.591
of mental health problems.

00:03:27.591 --> 00:03:29.890
I will then talk about
risk and resilience factors

00:03:29.890 --> 00:03:31.830
and the research we've done on that.

00:03:31.830 --> 00:03:34.380
Then also talk about the
dynamic nature of resilience,

00:03:34.380 --> 00:03:37.000
looking at the longitudinal analysis,

00:03:37.000 --> 00:03:40.310
and then have some findings
on the biological embedding

00:03:40.310 --> 00:03:41.453
of war exposure.

00:03:42.300 --> 00:03:46.629
And then I'll talk about the t-CETA study.

00:03:46.629 --> 00:03:47.462
This is about

00:03:47.462 --> 00:03:49.870
the phone-delivered
psychological intervention.

00:03:49.870 --> 00:03:51.250
I'll present some findings

00:03:51.250 --> 00:03:54.258
on the efficacy of
phone-delivered treatment,

00:03:54.258 --> 00:03:55.974
as well as the feasibility

00:03:55.974 --> 00:03:58.510
and acceptability of
phone-delivered treatment.

00:03:58.510 --> 00:04:01.176
And then I'll discuss those findings.

00:04:01.176 --> 00:04:04.120
I will discuss them as
I present some of them,

00:04:04.120 --> 00:04:06.283
but I'll be happy to be interrupted.

00:04:07.268 --> 00:04:10.040
And then I'll try to
bring everything together

00:04:10.040 --> 00:04:13.340
in the discussion and then have
some practical implications

00:04:13.340 --> 00:04:14.193
at the very end.

00:04:15.440 --> 00:04:20.190
I'd like to start, however,
with hearing from you.

00:04:20.190 --> 00:04:24.415
And I have prepared some
questions on Mentimeter.

00:04:24.415 --> 00:04:27.360
Obviously you're all experts,
but I'd like to know from you,

00:04:27.360 --> 00:04:30.390
how many refugees do you
think that there are currently

00:04:30.390 --> 00:04:31.320
across the world?

00:04:31.320 --> 00:04:36.320
And so if you go on menti.com
and enter that code,

00:04:36.430 --> 00:04:40.990
then you can enter your estimate

00:04:40.990 --> 00:04:43.840
of what you think how many
refugee children we have today

00:04:44.733 --> 00:04:45.566
across the world.

00:04:49.179 --> 00:04:50.650
So that can just be a wild guess

00:04:50.650 --> 00:04:52.150
to the best of your knowledge.

00:05:03.297 --> 00:05:04.923
I'll have some more questions after that.

00:05:06.222 --> 00:05:09.213
So it's worth logging in.

00:05:29.030 --> 00:05:31.170
Okay, you can still enter that,

00:05:31.170 --> 00:05:36.170
but I think most of the
answers are in the right area.

00:05:37.180 --> 00:05:39.930
So the most recent data suggests

00:05:40.839 --> 00:05:42.644
that there are about 85,000,

00:05:42.644 --> 00:05:44.420
85 million refugees in the world,

00:05:44.420 --> 00:05:46.330
half of which are children.

00:05:46.330 --> 00:05:50.090
So it is between 40 and 45 million.

00:05:50.090 --> 00:05:51.593
So you got that, right?

00:05:52.490 --> 00:05:54.263
So that was an easy question.

00:05:55.624 --> 00:05:57.000
The next question is a bit,

00:05:57.000 --> 00:05:59.337
might be a bit more challenging

00:05:59.337 --> 00:06:02.973
and because it's not, not
that obvious necessarily.

00:06:04.040 --> 00:06:06.280
So out of these children,

00:06:06.280 --> 00:06:07.900
how many do you think are suffering

00:06:07.900 --> 00:06:09.403
from mental health problems?

00:06:11.178 --> 00:06:12.011
In percentage.

00:06:14.664 --> 00:06:15.497
What's your guess?

00:06:21.915 --> 00:06:23.783
Well, we get a bit more of a range here,

00:06:26.425 --> 00:06:29.393
but the majority suggests 50%.

00:06:40.300 --> 00:06:41.173
That is,

00:06:42.810 --> 00:06:45.010
I think the whole range
that we see represented here

00:06:45.010 --> 00:06:48.180
is supported by the evidence.

00:06:48.180 --> 00:06:50.300
And I'll talk a bit more about that,

00:06:51.817 --> 00:06:53.167
and 50% is relatively high.

00:06:55.160 --> 00:06:56.780
Okay, and then the last question

00:06:57.780 --> 00:07:02.780
is the one regarding treatment.

00:07:03.280 --> 00:07:06.570
How many of these children
that have problems

00:07:06.570 --> 00:07:08.170
receive mental health treatment?

00:07:11.402 --> 00:07:13.152
So how many of the refugee children

00:07:14.026 --> 00:07:15.690
that actually have mental health problems

00:07:15.690 --> 00:07:18.623
end up receiving mental health treatment?

00:07:18.623 --> 00:07:19.643
What's your best guess?

00:07:23.400 --> 00:07:28.193
Okay, so yes, that looks,
that looks about right.

00:07:30.230 --> 00:07:32.460
We have quite a few refugee children

00:07:33.461 --> 00:07:35.130
that have mental health problems,

00:07:35.130 --> 00:07:39.620
and a very small number of
children actually receive help

00:07:39.620 --> 00:07:40.453
that they need.

00:07:42.158 --> 00:07:44.693
Great, so good responses,

00:07:45.570 --> 00:07:49.560
and I will provide data on
all these questions today,

00:07:49.560 --> 00:07:54.560
and yes, so you were quite
correct in that assessment.

00:07:58.920 --> 00:08:02.063
Some background on the
Syrian refugee crisis.

00:08:03.010 --> 00:08:04.300
So I'm not gonna go in great detail

00:08:04.300 --> 00:08:06.920
because most of you will
be very familiar with this,

00:08:06.920 --> 00:08:11.390
but this is a long, ongoing conflict.

00:08:11.390 --> 00:08:14.873
So it's already 10 years
that this has been going on,

00:08:14.873 --> 00:08:18.230
and it started with a conflict
between the Syrian government

00:08:18.230 --> 00:08:19.923
and a few rebel groups,

00:08:21.154 --> 00:08:26.110
and then turned into a
very, very complex proxy war

00:08:26.110 --> 00:08:27.990
involving various parties

00:08:27.990 --> 00:08:31.640
and has been in the news on
and off, not so much recently,

00:08:31.640 --> 00:08:33.410
but the conflict is still ongoing,

00:08:33.410 --> 00:08:36.593
although it's not the
same as it used to be.

00:08:38.810 --> 00:08:42.110
The consequences are substantial.

00:08:42.110 --> 00:08:47.110
So estimates talk about
half a million of deaths

00:08:47.740 --> 00:08:49.403
and many refugees.

00:08:50.875 --> 00:08:52.565
So there's 6.2 million Syrians

00:08:52.565 --> 00:08:53.840
that are displaced within Syria.

00:08:53.840 --> 00:08:56.127
Don't often hear about those.

00:08:56.127 --> 00:08:58.439
We hear mostly about those
externally displaced,

00:08:58.439 --> 00:09:00.430
which is about 5.6 million.

00:09:00.430 --> 00:09:03.160
And when we look at those,
so the most recent numbers,

00:09:03.160 --> 00:09:04.263
according to UNHCR,

00:09:05.647 --> 00:09:08.590
which these are the registered refugees,

00:09:08.590 --> 00:09:11.660
there are more refugees than
those that are registered,

00:09:11.660 --> 00:09:15.864
but there are 5.6 million
persons of concern.

00:09:15.864 --> 00:09:17.490
So these are registered with UNHCR,

00:09:17.490 --> 00:09:19.850
and most of them are in Turkey

00:09:19.850 --> 00:09:24.850
and then followed by
Lebanon, Jordan, Iraq, Egypt,

00:09:24.972 --> 00:09:26.553
and as well as some in North Africa.

00:09:27.590 --> 00:09:30.190
I think what's important to consider

00:09:30.190 --> 00:09:34.504
is that most of the refugees
remain in the Middle East.

00:09:34.504 --> 00:09:38.080
And that's obviously true
of most refugee settings

00:09:39.586 --> 00:09:41.600
that refugees tends to, well, be displaced

00:09:41.600 --> 00:09:43.507
within that country,

00:09:43.507 --> 00:09:46.265
and then end up in neighboring countries,

00:09:46.265 --> 00:09:48.668
although there's, in particular in Europe,

00:09:48.668 --> 00:09:51.430
there has been a lot of
debates and a lot of,

00:09:51.430 --> 00:09:53.405
at the moment, again,

00:09:53.405 --> 00:09:55.770
with the situation in Belarus and Poland

00:09:55.770 --> 00:09:58.368
with refugees coming over to Europe,

00:09:58.368 --> 00:10:01.166
but actually most of them
are in the Middle East.

00:10:01.166 --> 00:10:03.111
And it's only a small
proportion of the refugees

00:10:03.111 --> 00:10:04.033
that have fled to Europe.

00:10:04.968 --> 00:10:06.620
Importantly, for us, for my work,

00:10:06.620 --> 00:10:10.040
is that 50% of the Syrian
refugees are children,

00:10:10.040 --> 00:10:14.793
and they are significantly
affected by the crisis.

00:10:15.670 --> 00:10:19.670
One important aspect is
that they are often not,

00:10:19.670 --> 00:10:21.700
often don't have access to school,

00:10:21.700 --> 00:10:24.373
and that's as well the
case with our sample.

00:10:25.210 --> 00:10:26.710
So in our sample,

00:10:26.710 --> 00:10:30.113
with families that have been
in Lebanon for several years,

00:10:31.387 --> 00:10:34.030
about 40% of the children
have access to school.

00:10:34.030 --> 00:10:36.380
And, the others not, even
though they are in a,

00:10:37.431 --> 00:10:40.253
you know, in a relatively
stable refugee environment.

00:10:42.690 --> 00:10:44.210
What do we know about the mental health

00:10:44.210 --> 00:10:46.567
of Syrian refugee children?

00:10:46.567 --> 00:10:48.723
Well, there are more
studies more recently,

00:10:50.296 --> 00:10:53.250
and they actually suggest
that there's quite a range.

00:10:53.250 --> 00:10:55.010
So when we look at PTSD,

00:10:55.010 --> 00:10:59.863
the prevalence rates range
from 10% or 11% to 45.

00:11:02.309 --> 00:11:05.693
Similarly for anxiety
between about 10% to 50%.

00:11:05.693 --> 00:11:10.663
We see the same with
depression between 12 to 48%.

00:11:12.230 --> 00:11:15.173
So this suggests that
there is wide variability

00:11:15.173 --> 00:11:16.890
in those prevalence estimates.

00:11:16.890 --> 00:11:18.970
So we need to do more to find out

00:11:18.970 --> 00:11:22.963
what is actually the number
that we are looking at.

00:11:25.212 --> 00:11:26.380
And there is likely a significant need

00:11:26.380 --> 00:11:29.820
for mental health treatment
in Syrian refugee children.

00:11:29.820 --> 00:11:31.923
And I'll report more on that later.

00:11:33.760 --> 00:11:37.190
So it's obviously important to do research

00:11:37.190 --> 00:11:39.420
in order to understand the
mental health problems,

00:11:39.420 --> 00:11:43.390
as well as the needs of Syrian
refugee children, but also,

00:11:43.390 --> 00:11:45.720
and that's what we try
to do is to identify

00:11:46.592 --> 00:11:47.530
the specific factors and processes

00:11:47.530 --> 00:11:50.827
that are involved in risk and
resilience of these children.

00:11:50.827 --> 00:11:54.170
And that's important because
it would inform programs

00:11:54.170 --> 00:11:56.360
as well as interventions.

00:11:56.360 --> 00:12:01.360
And while we know quite a
bit about adverse events

00:12:01.925 --> 00:12:02.890
and negative outcomes,

00:12:02.890 --> 00:12:05.970
it's also important to
focus on protective factors

00:12:05.970 --> 00:12:08.473
because it might be easier to,

00:12:09.312 --> 00:12:11.640
or more feasible to
strengthen protective factors

00:12:11.640 --> 00:12:15.848
than prevent adverse
situations from happening.

00:12:15.848 --> 00:12:18.503
Because if there is a
conflict, it's ongoing,

00:12:18.503 --> 00:12:20.493
we can't resolve the conflict,
but we can strengthen what,

00:12:21.564 --> 00:12:23.045
you know, the protective factors

00:12:23.045 --> 00:12:24.433
amongst those children and families.

00:12:25.663 --> 00:12:29.010
And hopefully, you know, that
sort of research would help

00:12:29.010 --> 00:12:34.010
to also allocate resources to
those intervention services

00:12:34.330 --> 00:12:38.623
where they're most likely to
benefit the refugee children.

00:12:39.682 --> 00:12:43.300
There are three specific
factors that are important,

00:12:43.300 --> 00:12:45.770
that I think are important to consider

00:12:46.764 --> 00:12:47.884
in that sort of research,

00:12:47.884 --> 00:12:49.670
and the first one is developmental,

00:12:49.670 --> 00:12:52.940
that development occurs in nested contexts

00:12:54.084 --> 00:12:55.660
as proposed by Urie Bronfenbrenner

00:12:56.603 --> 00:12:57.903
in his Ecological Systems Theory.

00:12:58.861 --> 00:13:01.320
So when we look at an
individual developing,

00:13:01.320 --> 00:13:05.040
we always need to consider
the developmental context

00:13:05.040 --> 00:13:06.790
of that individual.

00:13:06.790 --> 00:13:11.400
And Bronfenbrenner suggested
that an individual is nested

00:13:11.400 --> 00:13:14.330
within a whole range of different systems.

00:13:14.330 --> 00:13:17.444
So most importantly
would be the microsystem.

00:13:17.444 --> 00:13:19.020
This is the immediate
context of the child,

00:13:19.020 --> 00:13:22.700
so that would be the family,
peers, the school environment,

00:13:22.700 --> 00:13:24.980
but these environments
are further influenced

00:13:24.980 --> 00:13:29.980
by the availability of
services on the exosystem,

00:13:30.134 --> 00:13:32.170
but also by cultural
facts in the macrosystem.

00:13:32.170 --> 00:13:34.650
So if you want to
understand the development

00:13:34.650 --> 00:13:35.550
of the individual,

00:13:35.550 --> 00:13:38.660
we have to also consider
all these different systems

00:13:38.660 --> 00:13:40.980
that represent the context

00:13:40.980 --> 00:13:43.510
within which the child is developing.

00:13:43.510 --> 00:13:46.950
But also the factors that then
have an impact on the context

00:13:48.077 --> 00:13:49.680
within the child is developing.

00:13:49.680 --> 00:13:51.183
So that's really important.

00:13:52.158 --> 00:13:54.070
Another important consideration
is that we can look

00:13:54.070 --> 00:13:57.150
at multiple levels of analysis.

00:13:57.150 --> 00:14:00.340
So in psychology, which is my background,

00:14:00.340 --> 00:14:02.495
we often look at the behavioral.

00:14:02.495 --> 00:14:03.730
We ask people to complete questionnaires

00:14:03.730 --> 00:14:05.520
or we interview them,

00:14:05.520 --> 00:14:09.820
but in order to really
understand the pathways

00:14:09.820 --> 00:14:11.740
across different levels of analysis,

00:14:11.740 --> 00:14:15.000
we also need to look at
the neurological level,

00:14:15.000 --> 00:14:19.117
at the biochemical level,
or at the molecular level

00:14:19.117 --> 00:14:22.343
at the far end, extremes
at the genetic level.

00:14:23.561 --> 00:14:25.711
And ideally we do all
of that to understand

00:14:27.010 --> 00:14:29.710
how they all influence each other

00:14:29.710 --> 00:14:31.780
and whether the behavioral
reflects what happens

00:14:31.780 --> 00:14:33.683
on the biological level and so forth.

00:14:35.188 --> 00:14:37.292
And the last point I already alluded to

00:14:37.292 --> 00:14:39.710
is that it's important to not just look at

00:14:39.710 --> 00:14:41.480
the absence of disease,

00:14:41.480 --> 00:14:45.170
but also at the presence
of positive outcomes.

00:14:45.170 --> 00:14:47.450
So there has been a lot of work

00:14:47.450 --> 00:14:51.270
that would focus on the
negative effects of risk factors

00:14:51.270 --> 00:14:52.250
and maladaptive outcomes.

00:14:52.250 --> 00:14:54.527
And we need to do more work

00:14:54.527 --> 00:14:56.129
to also look at protective factors

00:14:56.129 --> 00:14:57.935
and the positive effects
on adaptive outcomes.

00:14:57.935 --> 00:15:01.487
Obviously some work has been done on that,

00:15:01.487 --> 00:15:03.850
but I think it's really
important to not just,

00:15:03.850 --> 00:15:05.850
not forget that health is more

00:15:05.850 --> 00:15:07.500
than just the absence of disease.

00:15:08.620 --> 00:15:13.050
So we tried to do all of
that in the BIOPATH Study.

00:15:13.050 --> 00:15:16.460
And the BIOPATH Study is a collaboration

00:15:16.460 --> 00:15:19.950
between myself and colleagues
at Queen Mary University

00:15:20.928 --> 00:15:23.890
of London and Elie Karam
and colleagues at IDRAAC

00:15:23.890 --> 00:15:26.380
in Lebanon, and then our fieldwork

00:15:26.380 --> 00:15:27.893
partner was Médecins du Monde,

00:15:29.323 --> 00:15:30.673
and it was funded by NSCHE.

00:15:32.180 --> 00:15:36.290
So the focus of the
study is really looking

00:15:36.290 --> 00:15:38.950
at the individual differences
in response to war

00:15:38.950 --> 00:15:40.430
and displacement.

00:15:40.430 --> 00:15:41.992
We know that millions of children

00:15:41.992 --> 00:15:43.350
are affected by war and displacement,

00:15:43.350 --> 00:15:46.050
that many of them develop
psychological problems.

00:15:46.050 --> 00:15:50.350
However, some do not develop
psychological problems.

00:15:50.350 --> 00:15:51.833
They seem to be resilient.

00:15:52.810 --> 00:15:54.730
And this study is really
trying to understand

00:15:54.730 --> 00:15:58.130
the biological underpinnings
of such individual differences

00:15:58.130 --> 00:16:01.630
in response to war and displacement,

00:16:01.630 --> 00:16:04.410
taking into account multiple
settings, as I mentioned,

00:16:04.410 --> 00:16:07.600
with the biological systems theory,

00:16:07.600 --> 00:16:10.020
looking at the family
environment, school environment,

00:16:10.020 --> 00:16:13.136
the community, the neighborhoods,
availability of services,

00:16:13.136 --> 00:16:17.840
but also looking across
multiple levels of analysis.

00:16:17.840 --> 00:16:21.251
So looking at the
environmental factors, social,

00:16:21.251 --> 00:16:22.530
but also psychological, neuroendocrine,

00:16:22.530 --> 00:16:25.820
epigenetic and genetic factors.

00:16:25.820 --> 00:16:29.390
And then we look at both
risk and protective factors,

00:16:29.390 --> 00:16:32.190
both adaptive and maladaptive outcomes.

00:16:32.190 --> 00:16:35.150
I will not present findings

00:16:35.150 --> 00:16:36.910
about the adaptive outcomes today.

00:16:36.910 --> 00:16:39.758
I'm not going to talk
about wellbeing outcomes.

00:16:39.758 --> 00:16:41.310
Mostly we'll talk about
mental health outcomes,

00:16:41.310 --> 00:16:44.103
but we will consider both
risk and protective factors.

00:16:45.040 --> 00:16:48.198
And an important aspect of
this study is, you know,

00:16:48.198 --> 00:16:49.652
longitudinal design.

00:16:49.652 --> 00:16:51.510
It's very difficult to
do longitudinal research

00:16:51.510 --> 00:16:52.380
in these settings,

00:16:52.380 --> 00:16:55.050
or it's very challenging
to do any research

00:16:55.050 --> 00:16:56.650
in these settings,

00:16:56.650 --> 00:17:00.530
but longitudinal research
is particularly challenging.

00:17:00.530 --> 00:17:03.740
And so that's why many of the
studies are cross-sectional.

00:17:03.740 --> 00:17:07.380
And when I mentioned all these points,

00:17:07.380 --> 00:17:09.580
Catherine obviously collected data

00:17:09.580 --> 00:17:12.348
where she did all of those things as well

00:17:12.348 --> 00:17:13.750
and is one of the few studies

00:17:13.750 --> 00:17:16.640
that have achieved a longitudinal study,

00:17:16.640 --> 00:17:19.240
looking at multiple levels
of analysis and settings.

00:17:20.930 --> 00:17:24.490
The study design is that
basically we were interested in

00:17:24.490 --> 00:17:26.671
war exposure and displacement

00:17:26.671 --> 00:17:29.800
and how it has an impact on
both risk and resilience,

00:17:29.800 --> 00:17:33.860
captured with psychological
and biological outcomes.

00:17:33.860 --> 00:17:35.130
That's the main question.

00:17:35.130 --> 00:17:38.390
Then you want to understand
how environmental risk

00:17:38.390 --> 00:17:39.850
and protective factors

00:17:39.850 --> 00:17:41.760
outside of war exposure and displacement,

00:17:41.760 --> 00:17:44.870
as well as genetic factors
also have an impact

00:17:44.870 --> 00:17:48.720
on these outcomes and how
they moderate the impact

00:17:48.720 --> 00:17:51.670
that war exposure has on these outcomes.

00:17:51.670 --> 00:17:54.370
And then an important aspect of the study

00:17:54.370 --> 00:17:58.760
is to see whether the impact
of environmental risk factors,

00:17:58.760 --> 00:18:03.760
war exposure and genes, are
mediated through DNA methylation

00:18:05.094 --> 00:18:06.911
and impacted by genetic factors

00:18:06.911 --> 00:18:10.010
and further elements of risk
factors on those outcomes.

00:18:10.010 --> 00:18:11.414
So that's a baseline.

00:18:11.414 --> 00:18:12.430
And then we look at
all those factors again

00:18:12.430 --> 00:18:14.963
12 months later in the
followup assessment.

00:18:17.132 --> 00:18:22.132
We did that with a sample
of close to 1,600 families.

00:18:22.590 --> 00:18:25.914
So these are the children
plus their primary caregivers.

00:18:25.914 --> 00:18:28.400
So the total sample is therefore about,

00:18:28.400 --> 00:18:30.120
it's more than 3,000.

00:18:30.120 --> 00:18:31.800
The age range that we aimed for

00:18:31.800 --> 00:18:34.650
is between eight to 16 years.

00:18:34.650 --> 00:18:39.650
And we collected that data
in end of 2017, early 2018.

00:18:39.754 --> 00:18:42.900
And then a year later we
did a follow-up assessment,

00:18:42.900 --> 00:18:47.900
and we were aiming for about
two thirds of the sample.

00:18:48.170 --> 00:18:50.580
That's what we expected
we would be able to get,

00:18:50.580 --> 00:18:52.600
so we didn't actually have the funding

00:18:52.600 --> 00:18:55.347
to collect data from all.

00:18:55.347 --> 00:18:58.420
We would have been able to
collect a bit more than this,

00:18:58.420 --> 00:19:03.037
but there is about an
immobility of about two thirds.

00:19:03.037 --> 00:19:06.823
So within a year, a third of
the refugees tend to move on.

00:19:09.538 --> 00:19:11.080
A little bit about the
sample that we collected.

00:19:11.080 --> 00:19:14.300
So an important point is
that our recruitment rate

00:19:14.300 --> 00:19:17.056
was about 70%.

00:19:17.056 --> 00:19:19.580
That means 70% of all the
families that we approached

00:19:19.580 --> 00:19:21.473
for inclusion in the study said yes.

00:19:22.494 --> 00:19:26.880
So that suggests that our
sample is sort of representative

00:19:26.880 --> 00:19:30.333
of the refugee population
where we recruited from,

00:19:31.193 --> 00:19:33.973
and I'll share a bit more
details about the location.

00:19:35.250 --> 00:19:39.260
We ended up with a fairly
balanced gender split

00:19:39.260 --> 00:19:41.970
between female and male children.

00:19:41.970 --> 00:19:46.970
The age range ended up between
six to 19 years of age.

00:19:48.460 --> 00:19:51.950
We aimed for a broad age range

00:19:51.950 --> 00:19:53.350
because we wanted to look at

00:19:54.776 --> 00:19:56.124
developmental questions as well.

00:19:56.124 --> 00:19:56.980
We knew it would be very difficult

00:19:56.980 --> 00:19:59.914
to have more than two
waves of data collection.

00:19:59.914 --> 00:20:03.260
So we did sort of a compromise,
having a wide age range

00:20:03.260 --> 00:20:06.400
and then collecting data
longitudinally once,

00:20:06.400 --> 00:20:08.463
at two time points,

00:20:09.466 --> 00:20:12.313
which allows us to look
at longitudinal questions

00:20:12.313 --> 00:20:15.540
and taking the developmental
age of children

00:20:15.540 --> 00:20:17.080
into account as well.

00:20:17.080 --> 00:20:19.993
Most of them are Syrian, not surprisingly.

00:20:19.993 --> 00:20:23.010
The caregiver was in
most cases the mother,

00:20:23.010 --> 00:20:26.483
and in a small number fathers.

00:20:29.910 --> 00:20:33.260
The measures, we collected
a lot of different measures,

00:20:33.260 --> 00:20:36.260
so this is just a very simple summary.

00:20:36.260 --> 00:20:40.363
So we basically collected
data on child and caregiver,

00:20:41.850 --> 00:20:42.853
from child and caregiver.

00:20:42.853 --> 00:20:44.470
We collected data on
their psychopathology,

00:20:44.470 --> 00:20:45.770
on their wellbeing,

00:20:45.770 --> 00:20:49.630
and then a wide range of
protective and risk factors

00:20:50.620 --> 00:20:53.933
across the individual,
the social environment,

00:20:54.951 --> 00:20:57.793
and the wider social environments.

00:20:58.750 --> 00:21:02.046
And an important feature
is that we also collected

00:21:02.046 --> 00:21:05.441
biological samples, so we
have saliva that we collected

00:21:05.441 --> 00:21:09.230
for genetic analysis
and epigenetic analysis.

00:21:09.230 --> 00:21:10.770
Both of these are genome-wide.

00:21:10.770 --> 00:21:14.110
So we have genome-wide genetic data

00:21:14.110 --> 00:21:16.903
and epigenome-wide epigenetic data.

00:21:17.882 --> 00:21:18.893
So that's DNA methylation,

00:21:20.523 --> 00:21:22.700
and we also collected hair samples

00:21:22.700 --> 00:21:25.701
to look at three neuroendocrine variables.

00:21:25.701 --> 00:21:29.242
So most importantly
cortisol. (cross talking)

00:21:29.242 --> 00:21:32.217
- Michael, I have a couple
of clarification questions.

00:21:32.217 --> 00:21:34.212
Hi, this is (audio blurs).

00:21:34.212 --> 00:21:35.045
- Hello, good to see you.

00:21:35.045 --> 00:21:35.878
- Nice to see you too.

00:21:36.750 --> 00:21:40.050
The genetic data you
collected, only on the children

00:21:40.050 --> 00:21:41.813
or also the parents?

00:21:42.991 --> 00:21:44.314
- It's only the children.

00:21:44.314 --> 00:21:47.480
So the biological data is
only from the children.

00:21:47.480 --> 00:21:51.971
- And any reason why
you only did six to 19,

00:21:51.971 --> 00:21:53.363
rather than younger children?

00:21:54.420 --> 00:21:58.720
- The reason is that we
collected data from the children,

00:21:58.720 --> 00:22:01.046
and they needed to be able to respond

00:22:01.046 --> 00:22:05.711
basically to complete the data collection.

00:22:05.711 --> 00:22:08.500
The data was not
collected in written form.

00:22:08.500 --> 00:22:12.648
It was all in interview,
but the main reason is

00:22:12.648 --> 00:22:16.210
that we wanted to ensure that
our measures would be suitable

00:22:16.210 --> 00:22:17.990
for that age of children.

00:22:17.990 --> 00:22:21.400
So if we wanted to collect
data from younger children,

00:22:21.400 --> 00:22:24.728
we probably would have to use
different measures for them.

00:22:24.728 --> 00:22:25.913
- [Participant] Yeah.

00:22:25.913 --> 00:22:28.347
- So one of the challenges
is to find measures that work

00:22:28.347 --> 00:22:31.553
for an age range between eight and 18.

00:22:33.970 --> 00:22:37.450
- We could talk about it at
some point, but go ahead.

00:22:37.450 --> 00:22:38.283
- Yeah, great.

00:22:39.186 --> 00:22:40.019
Thank you.

00:22:41.809 --> 00:22:42.759
Okay, the location,

00:22:44.793 --> 00:22:47.450
you all probably know
where Lebanon is based.

00:22:47.450 --> 00:22:49.373
The important thing is that Lebanon

00:22:49.373 --> 00:22:53.705
is pretty much surrounded
by Syria, as you can see.

00:22:53.705 --> 00:22:55.980
So that's one of the reasons
why many Syrian refugees

00:22:55.980 --> 00:22:56.980
ended up in Lebanon.

00:22:58.591 --> 00:23:00.590
Within Lebanon, we collected data,

00:23:00.590 --> 00:23:02.923
which actually it's a
fairly small country.

00:23:03.840 --> 00:23:07.233
We collected all of our data
in the West Bekaa valley.

00:23:08.273 --> 00:23:12.420
So this is about an
hour, an hour and a half,

00:23:12.420 --> 00:23:16.540
away from Beirut by car.

00:23:16.540 --> 00:23:19.410
And some of the settlements
are fairly close to Syria,

00:23:19.410 --> 00:23:22.223
so maybe just a 10 minute
drive from the Syrian border.

00:23:23.733 --> 00:23:25.240
And we collected data

00:23:25.240 --> 00:23:28.313
from about 80 different
refugee settlements.

00:23:29.160 --> 00:23:32.894
And here is an example of one
of those refugee settlements.

00:23:32.894 --> 00:23:35.285
These are all informal settlements

00:23:35.285 --> 00:23:37.270
and relatively small settlements.

00:23:37.270 --> 00:23:40.106
Here is a typical one.

00:23:40.106 --> 00:23:42.820
You can see that they
have tented structures.

00:23:42.820 --> 00:23:45.404
They have satellite dishes on them.

00:23:45.404 --> 00:23:47.229
So they've been living there for,

00:23:47.229 --> 00:23:49.880
some of them for several
years already, but it's still,

00:23:49.880 --> 00:23:52.000
it's still a difficult life,

00:23:52.000 --> 00:23:56.240
even though they have relatively
solid tented structures.

00:23:56.240 --> 00:23:58.933
It does rain, and it
does snow in this region.

00:23:59.768 --> 00:24:01.710
And it's quite difficult living there.

00:24:01.710 --> 00:24:06.710
And here is the team that
collected data at wave one.

00:24:07.227 --> 00:24:08.670
So we had 20 field workers,

00:24:08.670 --> 00:24:11.670
two different teams that went
from settlement to settlement.

00:24:13.100 --> 00:24:16.193
All the data was collected in interviews,

00:24:17.060 --> 00:24:19.500
and we used tablets to do so.

00:24:19.500 --> 00:24:22.130
So the interviewer was asking

00:24:22.130 --> 00:24:26.145
all the questionnaire
questions directly to the child

00:24:26.145 --> 00:24:29.340
in Arabic, and then was
recording that in the tablets.

00:24:29.340 --> 00:24:33.590
Then we're uploading the data
the same day onto the service.

00:24:33.590 --> 00:24:36.647
So we basically had real time data.

00:24:36.647 --> 00:24:37.810
We always knew at the end of the day,

00:24:37.810 --> 00:24:39.303
what data has been collected.

00:24:40.507 --> 00:24:41.773
And then we're able to look at that data.

00:24:42.771 --> 00:24:44.970
At the same time as the
child was interviewed,

00:24:44.970 --> 00:24:48.010
another member of the team
was interviewing the caregiver

00:24:48.010 --> 00:24:51.750
in a different section of the tent

00:24:51.750 --> 00:24:54.126
or outside, which is the case here.

00:24:54.126 --> 00:24:59.126
And I have a little video
that provides a bit more

00:25:00.570 --> 00:25:02.850
of that context.

00:25:02.850 --> 00:25:04.007
So let me share that.

00:25:04.007 --> 00:25:07.820
So this, the sound is not
very loud, but it's in Arabic.

00:25:07.820 --> 00:25:09.670
So I don't know how many
of you speak Arabic.

00:25:09.670 --> 00:25:10.503
I don't.

00:25:11.450 --> 00:25:14.040
So it's translated into English,

00:25:14.040 --> 00:25:18.092
so you can read at the bottom
as the sound is not very loud,

00:25:18.092 --> 00:25:21.363
but hopefully you'll get a bit
of the context of this study.

00:25:25.850 --> 00:25:28.933
(speaking in Arabic)

00:27:20.320 --> 00:27:21.153
- All right.

00:27:21.153 --> 00:27:23.010
I hope you got a little bit of a flavor

00:27:23.010 --> 00:27:24.423
of the data collection.

00:27:25.378 --> 00:27:30.378
It was quite challenging
to get this study approved

00:27:30.560 --> 00:27:34.250
by all the different ethics committees,

00:27:34.250 --> 00:27:37.186
not so much in the UK, but in Lebanon.

00:27:37.186 --> 00:27:40.100
So it took quite a while
to get this through,

00:27:40.100 --> 00:27:42.880
and it was quite challenging
collecting the data,

00:27:42.880 --> 00:27:44.824
but the team did an excellent job,

00:27:44.824 --> 00:27:46.230
and we managed to collect,

00:27:46.230 --> 00:27:50.640
I would say very high quality
data on a large sample.

00:27:50.640 --> 00:27:52.720
So I will share some of these findings,

00:27:52.720 --> 00:27:55.260
much of that is still in the progress.

00:27:55.260 --> 00:27:58.900
So these are papers, none of
those papers are published yet,

00:27:58.900 --> 00:28:02.360
but either submitted in,

00:28:02.360 --> 00:28:06.860
on the review or they are
to be submitted very soon.

00:28:06.860 --> 00:28:11.460
So these are still sort of
somewhat preliminary findings

00:28:11.460 --> 00:28:12.673
in some cases.

00:28:14.020 --> 00:28:16.125
The first findings I'm going to present

00:28:16.125 --> 00:28:18.220
is about mental health
needs and treatment.

00:28:18.220 --> 00:28:22.021
And the research question
that we want to address

00:28:22.021 --> 00:28:23.620
with this analysis is the following:

00:28:23.620 --> 00:28:25.770
We wanted to look at
what is the prevalence

00:28:27.143 --> 00:28:28.803
of mental health problems
amongst our sample

00:28:28.803 --> 00:28:29.636
of Syrian refugee children.

00:28:29.636 --> 00:28:32.090
We wanted to look at what
are the main predictors

00:28:32.090 --> 00:28:33.690
of those mental health problems.

00:28:35.130 --> 00:28:37.260
We also wanted to look at
what is the perceived need

00:28:37.260 --> 00:28:40.102
for treatment of Syrian refugee children.

00:28:40.102 --> 00:28:41.920
And we asked the children
as well as the caregivers

00:28:41.920 --> 00:28:45.750
whether they think their
child would need treatment

00:28:45.750 --> 00:28:47.173
of mental health problems.

00:28:48.800 --> 00:28:52.023
And the last question, we
then also wanted to look at,

00:28:52.023 --> 00:28:53.178
what are common barriers

00:28:53.178 --> 00:28:54.300
to available mental health services?

00:28:54.300 --> 00:28:58.130
Asking the families whether
they are aware of any services

00:28:58.130 --> 00:29:00.350
available to them and
whether they would use them.

00:29:00.350 --> 00:29:01.973
And if not, why not?

00:29:02.880 --> 00:29:06.880
This particular paper is
the work of my post-docs,

00:29:06.880 --> 00:29:09.150
Fiona McEwen and Claudinei Biazoli.

00:29:10.870 --> 00:29:14.363
So a little bit about the measures here.

00:29:15.547 --> 00:29:18.809
So we used established self-report scales

00:29:18.809 --> 00:29:21.004
to measure mental health.

00:29:21.004 --> 00:29:23.384
So with depression, we used the CESD.

00:29:23.384 --> 00:29:25.008
For anxiety, we used the SCARED.

00:29:25.008 --> 00:29:26.408
Both of those were shortened

00:29:27.251 --> 00:29:30.310
to reduce the burden of data collection.

00:29:30.310 --> 00:29:31.730
They were reduced

00:29:31.730 --> 00:29:35.650
based on very carefully
conducted pilot studies.

00:29:35.650 --> 00:29:40.650
So we've basically collected
data on all of the items

00:29:40.830 --> 00:29:44.010
and then run the factor
analysis and reduced them

00:29:44.010 --> 00:29:45.563
to shorten them.

00:29:46.749 --> 00:29:48.330
The PTSD scale was the full scale,

00:29:48.330 --> 00:29:51.293
and externalizing problems
was the SDQ items,

00:29:52.252 --> 00:29:54.310
but we added some additional
items that we created

00:29:54.310 --> 00:29:57.923
based on DSM-5 for
conduct disorder and ODD.

00:29:59.440 --> 00:30:03.270
And all those were based on child reported

00:30:03.270 --> 00:30:05.650
except externalizing problems.

00:30:05.650 --> 00:30:07.070
When we collected externalizing problems

00:30:07.070 --> 00:30:10.254
based on child reports in pilot studies

00:30:10.254 --> 00:30:13.300
that data was not very reliable.

00:30:13.300 --> 00:30:16.250
So the other data seemed
to be more reliable

00:30:17.632 --> 00:30:19.508
on child report, but
externalizing problems

00:30:19.508 --> 00:30:20.603
was based on caregiver report.

00:30:21.933 --> 00:30:24.795
In addition, we conducted
clinical interviews

00:30:24.795 --> 00:30:26.693
in a sub sample with the many kids,

00:30:28.632 --> 00:30:31.520
and that was a sub sample of 134 children

00:30:31.520 --> 00:30:34.210
that were at different levels of risk

00:30:34.210 --> 00:30:37.940
in order to create a representative sample

00:30:37.940 --> 00:30:39.743
that represents the total sample.

00:30:41.314 --> 00:30:43.470
So we conducted those interviews in Arabic

00:30:44.570 --> 00:30:46.540
with the help of a local
clinical psychologist

00:30:46.540 --> 00:30:48.896
and two trained counselors,

00:30:48.896 --> 00:30:50.846
either in the clinic or in settlements.

00:30:51.980 --> 00:30:53.870
And then all those cases were discussed

00:30:53.870 --> 00:30:55.350
with the clinical supervisor

00:30:56.892 --> 00:30:58.442
before agreeing on a diagnosis.

00:31:01.011 --> 00:31:02.732
And here are the findings.

00:31:02.732 --> 00:31:06.357
So we have basically data
from clinical assessments

00:31:06.357 --> 00:31:08.310
in a sub sample of 134 children.

00:31:08.310 --> 00:31:13.310
We then created cutoff
scores based on that sample

00:31:13.352 --> 00:31:15.300
that we applied to the rest of the sample

00:31:15.300 --> 00:31:17.963
based on child self-report
and caregiver reports.

00:31:19.492 --> 00:31:21.130
But we also, as a third option,

00:31:21.130 --> 00:31:23.117
we asked the caregivers

00:31:23.117 --> 00:31:27.350
after asking them to
complete their self-report

00:31:27.350 --> 00:31:30.018
about their own mental
health, we asked them,

00:31:30.018 --> 00:31:32.390
do you think your child is suffering

00:31:32.390 --> 00:31:34.023
from those problems as well?

00:31:35.180 --> 00:31:40.180
So, and what we got, here
is the clinical example.

00:31:42.099 --> 00:31:43.790
We got relatively high levels,

00:31:43.790 --> 00:31:47.120
but within the margins
that all of you indicated

00:31:47.120 --> 00:31:48.600
at the very beginning.

00:31:48.600 --> 00:31:52.490
So post-traumatic stress
disorder was about 40%

00:31:52.490 --> 00:31:54.600
based on clinical interviews,

00:31:54.600 --> 00:31:59.230
externalizing problems around
27%, depression around 20%,

00:31:59.230 --> 00:32:04.230
anxiety disorders was very
high with close to 50%.

00:32:04.430 --> 00:32:09.430
And what is interesting is
that ADHD is not much higher

00:32:09.470 --> 00:32:14.433
than we would expect
in non-refugee samples.

00:32:15.420 --> 00:32:19.230
So it's particularly depression, PTSD,

00:32:19.230 --> 00:32:22.612
and anxiety disorders
that and externalizing,

00:32:22.612 --> 00:32:23.890
those that are particularly higher

00:32:25.085 --> 00:32:27.449
than it would be in other child samples.

00:32:27.449 --> 00:32:28.640
When we, based on those findings,

00:32:28.640 --> 00:32:31.820
then created cutoff scores
for our other measures

00:32:31.820 --> 00:32:33.940
and apply that to the whole sample.

00:32:33.940 --> 00:32:36.178
We basically found the same thing,

00:32:36.178 --> 00:32:38.035
which is not surprising
given that the cutoffs

00:32:38.035 --> 00:32:40.276
are based on the clinical sample,

00:32:40.276 --> 00:32:42.830
but that's basically replicated
across the whole sample.

00:32:42.830 --> 00:32:45.190
Actually the anxiety disorder measure

00:32:46.080 --> 00:32:48.130
was normally distributed,

00:32:48.130 --> 00:32:53.130
and we were very cautious
using the anxiety measure here

00:32:54.873 --> 00:32:58.890
because we don't think
it's a good measure.

00:32:58.890 --> 00:33:01.170
It's not a good measure to use

00:33:01.170 --> 00:33:03.710
to indicate mental health problems

00:33:03.710 --> 00:33:06.360
in this particular sample.

00:33:06.360 --> 00:33:08.810
The same measure that
we used with our cutoffs

00:33:08.810 --> 00:33:12.952
has been used by our colleagues in Turkey

00:33:12.952 --> 00:33:15.352
in a different, in a Syrian refugee sample

00:33:15.352 --> 00:33:16.185
living in Istanbul,

00:33:16.185 --> 00:33:19.320
and they found much
lower prevalence rates.

00:33:19.320 --> 00:33:21.317
And I think that our sample

00:33:21.317 --> 00:33:23.710
is of particular high vulnerability.

00:33:23.710 --> 00:33:26.370
So the third finding is
that we ask caregivers

00:33:26.370 --> 00:33:29.003
to report on what they think,

00:33:29.996 --> 00:33:31.697
whether their child needs help.

00:33:31.697 --> 00:33:36.040
And interestingly, we find
that it's fairly similar

00:33:36.040 --> 00:33:40.180
to our cutoff scores
and clinical interviews.

00:33:40.180 --> 00:33:45.180
So 35% of the caregivers thought
that their child needs help

00:33:45.600 --> 00:33:49.487
for PTSD or has significant PTSD problems.

00:33:49.487 --> 00:33:51.196
And with depression,

00:33:51.196 --> 00:33:52.897
it was a little bit
higher than the others.

00:33:52.897 --> 00:33:54.210
With anxiety disorder, it was a bit lower.

00:33:54.210 --> 00:33:56.250
We only asked them in relation to PTSD,

00:33:56.250 --> 00:33:58.283
depression, and anxiety.

00:33:59.150 --> 00:34:01.230
I think the most important, yes.

00:34:01.230 --> 00:34:02.660
- Sorry, if it's okay to jump in.

00:34:02.660 --> 00:34:04.110
I just have a question on that.

00:34:04.110 --> 00:34:05.630
So for the caregiver reports,

00:34:05.630 --> 00:34:08.230
are these whether they think

00:34:09.278 --> 00:34:10.578
that their child is experiencing this,

00:34:10.578 --> 00:34:11.890
or whether they, I guess, both

00:34:11.890 --> 00:34:13.460
think their child is experiencing this

00:34:13.460 --> 00:34:16.133
and should receive professional care?

00:34:17.480 --> 00:34:19.714
- Yes, I have to specify that.

00:34:19.714 --> 00:34:23.389
With this particular question
was whether their child has,

00:34:23.389 --> 00:34:24.723
is suffering from those problems.

00:34:25.749 --> 00:34:27.380
Basically we've,

00:34:27.380 --> 00:34:31.730
the caregivers completed
their own PTSD measure.

00:34:31.730 --> 00:34:34.790
Once they completed
their own PTSD measure,

00:34:34.790 --> 00:34:35.760
they were interviewed about that.

00:34:35.760 --> 00:34:36.990
They were also asked,

00:34:36.990 --> 00:34:40.000
is any other member in your family

00:34:40.000 --> 00:34:41.633
suffering from those problems?

00:34:42.650 --> 00:34:45.920
Is your child suffering
from problems like that?

00:34:45.920 --> 00:34:47.510
And then in a consequent question,

00:34:47.510 --> 00:34:50.365
we ask them about whether
the child needs help.

00:34:50.365 --> 00:34:53.005
So that's why I confused
those things, but yeah,

00:34:53.005 --> 00:34:55.470
so they were always asked,
the data that you see here

00:34:55.470 --> 00:34:57.780
is when they were asked
whether the child is suffering

00:34:57.780 --> 00:34:58.910
from similar problems

00:35:00.140 --> 00:35:02.390
as the ones that the
caregiver just reported.

00:35:05.829 --> 00:35:08.230
I think the main thing to
take away from this scale,

00:35:08.230 --> 00:35:10.300
from this table here is that

00:35:10.300 --> 00:35:13.630
when we look at any mental health problem,

00:35:13.630 --> 00:35:18.073
we look at prevalence rates close to 60%.

00:35:19.552 --> 00:35:20.385
So very high.

00:35:21.627 --> 00:35:25.067
Initially we looked at
the self-report scales,

00:35:25.067 --> 00:35:26.180
and we felt maybe that's
gonna be lower ones.

00:35:26.180 --> 00:35:29.769
We look at the clinical interview data,

00:35:29.769 --> 00:35:31.529
but even with clinical interviews,

00:35:31.529 --> 00:35:34.130
there were individual interviews
conducted by mental health,

00:35:34.130 --> 00:35:38.440
by trained people, we
get very similar scores.

00:35:38.440 --> 00:35:42.673
So a very high prevalence
of mental health problems.

00:35:44.050 --> 00:35:48.275
The next one is, finding
is looking at comorbidity.

00:35:48.275 --> 00:35:51.053
And I don't wanna say too
much about that, but this is,

00:35:52.315 --> 00:35:53.330
these are the comorbidity findings

00:35:53.330 --> 00:35:55.570
in the clinical sample, right?

00:35:55.570 --> 00:36:00.380
So we have the same as well
in with the self-report data,

00:36:00.380 --> 00:36:02.640
and findings look very similar.

00:36:02.640 --> 00:36:05.780
But the main message here
is as would be expected

00:36:05.780 --> 00:36:08.256
comorbidity is the norm.

00:36:08.256 --> 00:36:13.256
So in almost 90% of the
cases, we have comorbidity,

00:36:13.510 --> 00:36:16.563
usually involving PTSD and something else.

00:36:17.470 --> 00:36:19.840
- Sorry, do you mind if I just ask quickly

00:36:19.840 --> 00:36:24.574
what the rates in the
previous table were in Turkey

00:36:24.574 --> 00:36:26.488
and whether you think this?

00:36:26.488 --> 00:36:27.509
- Oh, in Turkey.

00:36:27.509 --> 00:36:29.380
- Yeah, and also whether you think

00:36:29.380 --> 00:36:32.340
that the discrepancies
indicate differences

00:36:33.800 --> 00:36:37.710
in prior experiences before
arriving to the country

00:36:37.710 --> 00:36:40.520
or because of environmental factors

00:36:40.520 --> 00:36:43.303
or just like the income of that family?

00:36:44.460 --> 00:36:48.820
- Yeah, so I don't, I don't
remember the exact numbers,

00:36:48.820 --> 00:36:53.820
but where we found about 50% of children

00:36:54.220 --> 00:36:57.230
are above the cutoff scores for anxiety.

00:36:57.230 --> 00:37:01.863
In the Turkish sample it was
I think between five and 10%,

00:37:03.804 --> 00:37:04.954
so significantly lower.

00:37:06.077 --> 00:37:07.060
And we think, so we,

00:37:07.060 --> 00:37:09.430
this is still something
that we have to look at,

00:37:09.430 --> 00:37:13.600
but we think that our
sample probably, you know,

00:37:13.600 --> 00:37:17.870
are living in very much
more difficult context

00:37:17.870 --> 00:37:22.493
and may have more of higher war exposure,

00:37:23.518 --> 00:37:24.930
but we haven't compared that.

00:37:24.930 --> 00:37:27.020
So I can't say whether that's the case,

00:37:27.020 --> 00:37:30.920
but what seems to be the
case is that the context

00:37:30.920 --> 00:37:32.620
that they're living in right now

00:37:32.620 --> 00:37:37.063
might be much more challenging
in Lebanon than in Turkey.

00:37:38.524 --> 00:37:40.020
And that might explain some of that.

00:37:40.020 --> 00:37:42.150
- Yeah, that's certainly
been my understanding

00:37:42.150 --> 00:37:44.100
as someone that has done work in Turkey

00:37:45.118 --> 00:37:45.951
and seen the statistics in Lebanon.

00:37:45.951 --> 00:37:48.790
It's the conditions are way
worse, but then of course, I,

00:37:48.790 --> 00:37:51.581
you know, you're always curious about,

00:37:51.581 --> 00:37:54.180
you understand that a lot of that

00:37:54.180 --> 00:37:56.750
is from the current
environmental circumstances,

00:37:56.750 --> 00:37:59.640
but then you also wonder how
much of this also relates

00:37:59.640 --> 00:38:01.820
to the prior circumstances.

00:38:01.820 --> 00:38:03.060
Thank you.

00:38:03.060 --> 00:38:03.893
- Sure.

00:38:04.901 --> 00:38:06.544
Well, actually my next slide addresses

00:38:06.544 --> 00:38:09.164
some of those questions because
we look at the predictors,

00:38:09.164 --> 00:38:10.296
and that's a busy table.

00:38:10.296 --> 00:38:14.260
So I just wanted to communicate
the main message here.

00:38:14.260 --> 00:38:18.083
So we looked at demographic
factors or events,

00:38:18.083 --> 00:38:20.184
perceived refugee
environment is the quality

00:38:20.184 --> 00:38:21.080
of the current environment.

00:38:21.080 --> 00:38:26.080
That includes access to
facilities, living conditions,

00:38:26.163 --> 00:38:30.302
the social context within
the family and the community,

00:38:30.302 --> 00:38:31.835
so a whole range of different factors.

00:38:31.835 --> 00:38:34.480
This is a measure that
we created from scratch

00:38:34.480 --> 00:38:37.003
based on various pilot studies.

00:38:38.841 --> 00:38:40.924
And we also looked at child/caregiver,

00:38:40.924 --> 00:38:43.243
child relationship and caregiver
mental health symptoms.

00:38:44.136 --> 00:38:46.140
And what you can see is that
war events are important.

00:38:46.140 --> 00:38:49.083
They predict all the
outcomes that we looked at,

00:38:50.445 --> 00:38:51.810
but actually the strongest predictor

00:38:51.810 --> 00:38:54.000
was the current environment.

00:38:54.000 --> 00:38:56.860
And so the higher the score,

00:38:56.860 --> 00:38:59.180
the higher the quality
of their environment.

00:38:59.180 --> 00:39:02.930
So that's why you see the
negative relationships here,

00:39:02.930 --> 00:39:05.740
but the effects of the current environment

00:39:06.580 --> 00:39:11.270
is much higher than that of war events.

00:39:11.270 --> 00:39:14.540
An important predictor was
also maltreatment in the family

00:39:15.463 --> 00:39:20.133
and also caregiver mental health problems.

00:39:20.990 --> 00:39:24.680
- Michael, could I, could
I ask you a question?

00:39:24.680 --> 00:39:29.680
Until we find out how to
get this person miked up.

00:39:30.105 --> 00:39:33.161
And I know you may be able to say that,

00:39:33.161 --> 00:39:35.037
but I'm just going to ask it quickly.

00:39:35.037 --> 00:39:36.630
The maltreatment question is obviously

00:39:36.630 --> 00:39:39.002
an extremely sensitive question

00:39:39.002 --> 00:39:40.440
to ask the families and caregivers.

00:39:40.440 --> 00:39:43.580
So, and it's one that's really
interesting to many scholars.

00:39:43.580 --> 00:39:45.883
Can you just elaborate a little bit

00:39:45.883 --> 00:39:47.223
about how you get these data?

00:39:48.501 --> 00:39:51.140
- So this is the ICAST
measure that we used,

00:39:51.140 --> 00:39:56.140
and we, so Elie Karam, who
was a co-peer on the study,

00:39:57.990 --> 00:39:59.433
they've used that same
measure in previous studies

00:39:59.433 --> 00:40:02.050
in similar samples.

00:40:02.050 --> 00:40:04.750
So we knew a little bit
from their experience

00:40:04.750 --> 00:40:07.850
what questions would be more
acceptable and which less.

00:40:07.850 --> 00:40:09.600
And we excluded certain questions

00:40:09.600 --> 00:40:11.357
that would be unethical to ask.

00:40:12.210 --> 00:40:13.560
- [Catherine] Okay.

00:40:13.560 --> 00:40:18.560
- But I think important to
maybe realize here is that

00:40:20.049 --> 00:40:24.700
relatively harsh parenting
is much more accepted

00:40:24.700 --> 00:40:27.523
in that cultural context
than compared to here.

00:40:28.779 --> 00:40:31.830
So it often felt that neither the child,

00:40:31.830 --> 00:40:33.180
the children or the parents

00:40:34.886 --> 00:40:36.132
would necessarily find it unusual

00:40:36.132 --> 00:40:37.578
to report some of the behaviors

00:40:37.578 --> 00:40:39.333
that we would find a
bit more alarming here.

00:40:40.771 --> 00:40:41.604
- Maybe you could continue,

00:40:41.604 --> 00:40:44.189
a question from the room is going to try

00:40:44.189 --> 00:40:47.260
and come through the
chat via various people.

00:40:47.260 --> 00:40:50.650
- Yeah, so then a very
important finding is

00:40:50.650 --> 00:40:53.270
when we asked them, the caregivers,

00:40:53.270 --> 00:40:54.850
whether they are interested

00:40:54.850 --> 00:40:57.800
in receiving mental health
treatment for the child,

00:40:57.800 --> 00:41:02.690
that was only 25% or 25.6%.

00:41:02.690 --> 00:41:07.160
So there's quite a disparity
between the children

00:41:07.160 --> 00:41:08.590
that seem to be at risk

00:41:08.590 --> 00:41:11.260
of significant mental health problems

00:41:11.260 --> 00:41:14.390
and the caregivers that
felt that their child

00:41:14.390 --> 00:41:15.843
would benefit from treatment.

00:41:16.990 --> 00:41:20.210
So we then asked them about
the awareness of services,

00:41:20.210 --> 00:41:23.780
and less than 50% were
aware of any services.

00:41:23.780 --> 00:41:25.673
We asked them if they've used any,

00:41:26.822 --> 00:41:30.210
and only very few used any
of the available services.

00:41:30.210 --> 00:41:32.930
The majority of services
used where those delivered

00:41:32.930 --> 00:41:34.830
in the communities.

00:41:34.830 --> 00:41:37.091
And then we asked them about reasons

00:41:37.091 --> 00:41:39.151
why they are not using services.

00:41:39.151 --> 00:41:40.451
Most of them said, well,

00:41:40.451 --> 00:41:41.867
they don't have a need for treatment.

00:41:41.867 --> 00:41:43.384
And for those that have
a need for treatment

00:41:43.384 --> 00:41:45.900
but still did not use
them, the reasons were

00:41:45.900 --> 00:41:48.230
that they were not aware
of services or didn't know,

00:41:48.230 --> 00:41:50.123
did not know how to access them,

00:41:51.066 --> 00:41:52.320
thought that they were too expensive.

00:41:52.320 --> 00:41:54.350
There were transport issues.

00:41:54.350 --> 00:41:57.508
Lebanon doesn't really
have public transport,

00:41:57.508 --> 00:41:58.660
so that's a big challenge.

00:41:58.660 --> 00:42:01.550
And then a few people
also mentioned stigma

00:42:02.440 --> 00:42:03.973
and security issues.

00:42:06.620 --> 00:42:10.800
So I have quite a few
other findings to share.

00:42:10.800 --> 00:42:12.630
So I'd like to move on,

00:42:12.630 --> 00:42:14.447
but I'll come back to
some of those findings

00:42:14.447 --> 00:42:16.044
and what they could mean.

00:42:16.044 --> 00:42:20.390
So the second set of,
well, actually two studies

00:42:20.390 --> 00:42:22.113
is looking at risk and resilience.

00:42:23.033 --> 00:42:25.511
And the question that we were
addressing here is firstly,

00:42:25.511 --> 00:42:28.211
you wanted to see how many
Syrian refugee children

00:42:28.211 --> 00:42:30.993
could we consider as
resilient within that sample.

00:42:31.967 --> 00:42:33.270
Second question, what
factors differentiates

00:42:33.270 --> 00:42:35.900
the resilient children from those at risk?

00:42:35.900 --> 00:42:39.410
And the third question, how
does resilience change over time

00:42:39.410 --> 00:42:41.860
and what predicts those changes?

00:42:41.860 --> 00:42:46.050
And then what is the directionality
of detected associations

00:42:46.050 --> 00:42:48.823
when looking at development
of resilience over time?

00:42:50.240 --> 00:42:53.110
And this is the work
conducted by my PhD student,

00:42:53.110 --> 00:42:54.163
Cassandra Popham,

00:42:55.364 --> 00:42:58.023
who is in the process of
writing up all these findings.

00:42:59.445 --> 00:43:04.200
So the first question
is what is resilience

00:43:04.200 --> 00:43:06.410
and how do we define resilience?

00:43:06.410 --> 00:43:08.300
Lots of people define
resilience in different ways.

00:43:08.300 --> 00:43:12.630
So we stuck to a fairly simple definition.

00:43:12.630 --> 00:43:15.360
Resilience is positive
development despite adversity.

00:43:15.360 --> 00:43:19.080
So basically refugee
children that experienced war

00:43:19.080 --> 00:43:22.086
but did not develop
mental health problems.

00:43:22.086 --> 00:43:23.663
That's our definition of resilience.

00:43:24.881 --> 00:43:29.763
And we did that across
multiple dimensions.

00:43:30.713 --> 00:43:31.690
So many studies that look at resilience

00:43:31.690 --> 00:43:34.863
may only look at depression
or only look at PTSD.

00:43:35.989 --> 00:43:38.149
But we said a resilient child

00:43:38.149 --> 00:43:39.410
is one that is below the cutoffs

00:43:39.410 --> 00:43:43.260
of these three outcomes
here, below depression,

00:43:43.260 --> 00:43:46.400
PTSD and externalizing problems.

00:43:46.400 --> 00:43:49.830
And that was about 20% of the sample.

00:43:50.820 --> 00:43:54.594
So 20% were below these three cutoffs.

00:43:54.594 --> 00:43:56.133
We didn't consider anxiety

00:43:56.133 --> 00:43:57.420
because anxiety didn't differentiate well

00:43:57.420 --> 00:43:58.913
between risk and resilience.

00:44:01.550 --> 00:44:05.707
If a child was above one or more cutoffs,

00:44:05.707 --> 00:44:08.107
they were considered in
the risk, at risk group.

00:44:09.513 --> 00:44:11.763
And that was the remaining 80%.

00:44:13.490 --> 00:44:16.750
So we had about 20% that
we considered resilient,

00:44:16.750 --> 00:44:19.713
and 80 at risk for mental health problems.

00:44:21.651 --> 00:44:24.410
In the next step, we look at war exposure,

00:44:24.410 --> 00:44:29.410
and you can, war exposure
was measured with a scale

00:44:29.410 --> 00:44:31.518
with 25 items.

00:44:31.518 --> 00:44:34.090
So usually that's used by just summing up

00:44:34.090 --> 00:44:36.993
all those different war
events in the total scale.

00:44:38.160 --> 00:44:41.121
But we wanted to look more
carefully at war exposure.

00:44:41.121 --> 00:44:43.270
And we also, in addition
to the total score

00:44:43.270 --> 00:44:45.850
looked at sort of the different types

00:44:45.850 --> 00:44:47.440
that children experience,

00:44:47.440 --> 00:44:49.990
the different types of war
exposure that they have.

00:44:51.324 --> 00:44:54.210
So there was a small number of children

00:44:54.210 --> 00:44:57.230
that didn't report any war exposure.

00:44:57.230 --> 00:45:00.700
Important as well, war exposure
was based on child report

00:45:00.700 --> 00:45:02.563
and caregiver report.

00:45:03.485 --> 00:45:05.893
If any of them reported a war event,

00:45:07.224 --> 00:45:10.466
then we considered them as happened.

00:45:10.466 --> 00:45:13.570
So it was the combination of
child and caregiver reports.

00:45:13.570 --> 00:45:15.570
So there was some children

00:45:15.570 --> 00:45:18.265
predominantly experienced bombardment,

00:45:18.265 --> 00:45:19.763
but not any of the others.

00:45:19.763 --> 00:45:22.370
So they were in that theoretical
group of bombardment,

00:45:22.370 --> 00:45:26.863
and the most, most severe was bodily harm.

00:45:28.046 --> 00:45:29.530
Basically if children
experienced bodily harm,

00:45:29.530 --> 00:45:32.833
they experienced most of
the other events as well.

00:45:33.810 --> 00:45:36.110
We created those different groupings

00:45:37.323 --> 00:45:40.143
in order to match children
based on their war exposure.

00:45:42.339 --> 00:45:45.770
So we matched, we selected
the resilient children,

00:45:45.770 --> 00:45:47.748
all of the resilient children

00:45:47.748 --> 00:45:50.146
and tried to match each of these children

00:45:50.146 --> 00:45:53.593
with one that is at risk,
so is in the risk group,

00:45:54.977 --> 00:45:56.926
but has similar war exposure.

00:45:56.926 --> 00:45:58.860
So we matched them, for
each resilient child,

00:45:58.860 --> 00:46:03.580
we tried to find one child
that has the same total score

00:46:03.580 --> 00:46:07.027
in war exposure, but also
the same theoretical group.

00:46:07.027 --> 00:46:10.520
So for example, this child
here is in the resilient group

00:46:10.520 --> 00:46:15.023
and experienced bodily harm
and has a fairly high score.

00:46:16.065 --> 00:46:17.610
We matched that child with another child

00:46:17.610 --> 00:46:19.970
that is in risk group that has high,

00:46:19.970 --> 00:46:23.860
that has mental health problems
and similar war exposure

00:46:25.365 --> 00:46:28.285
in order to look at individual differences

00:46:28.285 --> 00:46:31.140
and what predicts why are some children

00:46:31.140 --> 00:46:32.640
that have the same war exposure

00:46:32.640 --> 00:46:36.100
than children that have
mental health problems,

00:46:36.100 --> 00:46:38.300
why do they not have
mental health problems?

00:46:40.540 --> 00:46:43.405
So then we conducted logistic regressions

00:46:43.405 --> 00:46:45.202
to identify the factors that differentiate

00:46:45.202 --> 00:46:46.673
between risk and resilience.

00:46:47.820 --> 00:46:49.970
So basically we have children

00:46:49.970 --> 00:46:51.963
that have identical war exposure.

00:46:53.125 --> 00:46:55.475
Some children developed
mental health problems.

00:46:56.606 --> 00:46:57.439
Others did not.

00:46:58.321 --> 00:46:59.610
And we wanted to see what
factors differentiate

00:46:59.610 --> 00:47:01.220
between the two.

00:47:01.220 --> 00:47:03.420
And so we looked at a
whole range of factors

00:47:04.637 --> 00:47:05.470
as you can see here.

00:47:06.690 --> 00:47:08.140
So these are the odds ratios.

00:47:12.630 --> 00:47:14.460
We all, we conducted individual models

00:47:14.460 --> 00:47:17.070
for each of these predictors,

00:47:17.070 --> 00:47:18.460
but then also combined them

00:47:19.491 --> 00:47:21.309
and corrected for multiple corrections.

00:47:21.309 --> 00:47:24.546
So the dark blue ones are the
ones that survived corrections

00:47:24.546 --> 00:47:26.284
at all, we'll have a look at those.

00:47:26.284 --> 00:47:27.117
So self-esteem was one of them.

00:47:27.117 --> 00:47:29.520
Self-esteem might be a correlate

00:47:29.520 --> 00:47:32.140
of good mental health, right?

00:47:32.140 --> 00:47:35.427
So we don't really say with this analysis

00:47:35.427 --> 00:47:37.668
whether this is a
correlate or a predictor.

00:47:37.668 --> 00:47:39.260
It's just something that
differentiates this group.

00:47:39.260 --> 00:47:41.530
Children in the resilience group

00:47:41.530 --> 00:47:44.003
tend to have higher self-esteem.

00:47:45.120 --> 00:47:48.260
Children in the risk group,
tend to have caregivers

00:47:48.260 --> 00:47:50.828
with more PTSD problems,

00:47:50.828 --> 00:47:53.570
experienced more child maltreatments,

00:47:53.570 --> 00:47:56.386
have caregivers with high depression,

00:47:56.386 --> 00:47:59.050
experience more loneliness,

00:47:59.050 --> 00:48:02.629
and also have higher scores
in environmental sensitivity.

00:48:02.629 --> 00:48:03.880
This is a temperament trait

00:48:04.731 --> 00:48:07.863
that reflects heightened
sensitivity to the environment,

00:48:08.726 --> 00:48:11.207
both negative and positive experiences.

00:48:11.207 --> 00:48:16.207
So this is what we found,
and then the next study,

00:48:16.270 --> 00:48:19.070
we wanted to look at the
change in resilience over time.

00:48:20.189 --> 00:48:21.913
And this is a quite a busy figure here,

00:48:22.848 --> 00:48:25.003
but basically we have the baseline,

00:48:26.348 --> 00:48:28.520
and we have the follow up data.

00:48:28.520 --> 00:48:30.853
At baseline, that's
just what I showed you.

00:48:31.707 --> 00:48:34.870
20% are in the resilience group,

00:48:34.870 --> 00:48:37.600
and the remaining 80%
in the high risk group.

00:48:37.600 --> 00:48:41.500
When we look at those groupings,
again at the followup,

00:48:41.500 --> 00:48:44.326
we do see that now at the followup,

00:48:44.326 --> 00:48:46.360
there's 30% in the resilient group,

00:48:46.360 --> 00:48:50.103
and about 66% in the high risk group.

00:48:51.151 --> 00:48:54.240
However, there is
considerable change over time.

00:48:54.240 --> 00:48:59.240
There is a group that is
stable, stably resilient,

00:49:00.789 --> 00:49:04.766
but there is a significance,
about half of the children,

00:49:04.766 --> 00:49:06.460
that are in the resilient
group at wave one,

00:49:06.460 --> 00:49:09.403
are no longer in the
resilient group at wave two.

00:49:10.443 --> 00:49:13.380
So that means they had
a significant increase

00:49:13.380 --> 00:49:15.267
in mental health problems.

00:49:15.267 --> 00:49:17.267
And we define that at least a 20% change

00:49:18.338 --> 00:49:20.404
in one of the mental health outcomes.

00:49:20.404 --> 00:49:22.990
So they are above at least one cutoff

00:49:22.990 --> 00:49:25.728
and at least 20% changed.

00:49:25.728 --> 00:49:27.493
So it's not just a minimal change,

00:49:28.427 --> 00:49:30.140
just slightly above the cutoff.

00:49:30.140 --> 00:49:32.363
These are significant changes.

00:49:32.363 --> 00:49:36.140
On average the change that
we saw is 40% increase.

00:49:36.140 --> 00:49:38.842
- So, Michael, I have another question

00:49:38.842 --> 00:49:40.542
to try to understand this picture.

00:49:41.761 --> 00:49:43.980
So the definition of risk here

00:49:45.545 --> 00:49:48.763
is if they've been exposed to war.

00:49:49.890 --> 00:49:52.425
- No, the definition of risk here

00:49:52.425 --> 00:49:55.043
is being above at least one of the cutoffs

00:49:55.043 --> 00:49:56.283
of our three mental health outcomes.

00:50:00.090 --> 00:50:02.910
- So, Michael, can I ask
you a question as well?

00:50:03.843 --> 00:50:05.908
And you also have a question in the chat.

00:50:05.908 --> 00:50:08.900
You know, we've discussed that,

00:50:08.900 --> 00:50:10.830
given that our work for instance

00:50:11.686 --> 00:50:13.504
is actually measuring resilience

00:50:13.504 --> 00:50:16.243
as opposed to assuming it's defacto

00:50:16.243 --> 00:50:19.590
from the presence or absence
of mental health symptoms,

00:50:19.590 --> 00:50:22.183
there was a reason why
you did not do that.

00:50:22.183 --> 00:50:24.460
And that's probably because you
work in a younger age group,

00:50:24.460 --> 00:50:29.460
but I'm just wondering why you
call them resilience and risk

00:50:29.960 --> 00:50:32.733
instead of calling them
just low-risk and high-risk.

00:50:33.747 --> 00:50:37.260
In other words, why do you
label them resilient children

00:50:38.213 --> 00:50:39.520
because that is not measuring resilience,

00:50:39.520 --> 00:50:41.470
it's measuring the absence of symptoms.

00:50:43.090 --> 00:50:47.413
- Yeah, so, these are all valid points.

00:50:48.308 --> 00:50:50.700
So we refer to them as resilient

00:50:51.600 --> 00:50:54.130
when we matched them on the war exposure

00:50:54.130 --> 00:50:55.551
because they had war exposure,

00:50:55.551 --> 00:50:57.677
but they did not develop
mental health problems.

00:50:57.677 --> 00:50:58.540
In the current analysis actually,

00:50:58.540 --> 00:51:00.220
we talk about low and high risk

00:51:01.055 --> 00:51:03.453
because they're not
matched to war exposure.

00:51:04.620 --> 00:51:06.476
Here we just look at low,

00:51:06.476 --> 00:51:09.080
basically low mental health
and high mental health health.

00:51:09.080 --> 00:51:11.810
- But the word exposure definition,

00:51:11.810 --> 00:51:16.260
it refers to events prior to the baseline.

00:51:16.260 --> 00:51:18.256
- Yes, yep.

00:51:18.256 --> 00:51:23.256
- Could it be that the children
that go from blue to pink

00:51:25.460 --> 00:51:28.200
is because they're experiencing new,

00:51:28.200 --> 00:51:31.820
so it's not much driven
by their resilience,

00:51:31.820 --> 00:51:34.219
but or changing their resilience

00:51:34.219 --> 00:51:38.080
but additional environmental factors like.

00:51:38.080 --> 00:51:40.930
- Yeah, exactly, exactly.

00:51:40.930 --> 00:51:43.250
That's, that's an important,

00:51:43.250 --> 00:51:44.580
it's important to realize

00:51:44.580 --> 00:51:47.580
that even though maybe when
we look at war exposure,

00:51:47.580 --> 00:51:50.510
some children seem to be resilient.

00:51:50.510 --> 00:51:55.130
The context of displacement
itself leads to new trauma.

00:51:55.130 --> 00:52:00.130
And even though a child might've
been resilient at one point

00:52:00.179 --> 00:52:02.470
doesn't necessarily mean that
child will remain resilient

00:52:02.470 --> 00:52:05.619
or will remain at low
risk of mental health,

00:52:05.619 --> 00:52:08.400
even in that context of
displacement, we see changes.

00:52:08.400 --> 00:52:10.599
Most of the change though

00:52:10.599 --> 00:52:12.830
is from children being at higher risk

00:52:12.830 --> 00:52:14.493
to no longer be at higher risk,

00:52:15.679 --> 00:52:19.380
but it's really a dynamic
development over time

00:52:19.380 --> 00:52:22.203
where the majority of
children don't really change,

00:52:24.164 --> 00:52:25.204
but some children change,

00:52:25.204 --> 00:52:27.400
but not only from higher risk to low risk,

00:52:27.400 --> 00:52:30.040
but also from lower risk to high risk.

00:52:30.040 --> 00:52:32.014
And I think that's an important message

00:52:32.014 --> 00:52:35.620
that we don't think that once
we measure mental health,

00:52:35.620 --> 00:52:38.490
and it's low, then it will stay like that

00:52:38.490 --> 00:52:41.100
because they're living in
a very challenging context

00:52:41.100 --> 00:52:43.063
where they experience neutral as well,

00:52:44.094 --> 00:52:44.927
that there is considerable change.

00:52:44.927 --> 00:52:46.984
We actually looked in that paper,

00:52:46.984 --> 00:52:49.909
we look up the predictors for
all of these different groups.

00:52:49.909 --> 00:52:53.587
And I just mentioned some of
the stable high and stable low.

00:52:53.587 --> 00:52:55.993
So they're fairly similar
to what I showed earlier.

00:52:57.206 --> 00:52:59.210
So it is caregiver mental health.

00:52:59.210 --> 00:53:01.120
It's maltreatment.

00:53:01.120 --> 00:53:05.144
It's aspects of the family environment,

00:53:05.144 --> 00:53:07.660
such as parent conflict
or maternal acceptance,

00:53:07.660 --> 00:53:09.646
and then individual factors

00:53:09.646 --> 00:53:12.820
such as sensitivity being
higher in those at higher risk.

00:53:12.820 --> 00:53:15.108
And self-esteem being higher

00:53:15.108 --> 00:53:17.108
in those that are in the low risk group.

00:53:18.710 --> 00:53:20.250
- Michael, you have two questions.

00:53:20.250 --> 00:53:22.207
So I just want to reiterate,

00:53:22.207 --> 00:53:24.147
it's really important
to know that trajectory

00:53:24.147 --> 00:53:26.960
has been malleable over time
and that's why intervention is,

00:53:26.960 --> 00:53:31.200
you know, really important
at that kind of age group.

00:53:31.200 --> 00:53:33.150
So thank you for that message.

00:53:33.150 --> 00:53:34.100
You have two questions.

00:53:34.100 --> 00:53:35.440
If I can ask you quickly,

00:53:35.440 --> 00:53:37.310
Candace (audio blurs) has a question

00:53:38.869 --> 00:53:41.310
on how was war events
measured in these analysis?

00:53:41.310 --> 00:53:44.480
And you have another question
from the person in the room

00:53:45.440 --> 00:53:48.807
on the symptomology when
they report a symptomology

00:53:48.807 --> 00:53:51.690
of mental illnesses, whether they were,

00:53:51.690 --> 00:53:52.850
I think you answered that.

00:53:52.850 --> 00:53:55.030
Were caregiver educated?

00:53:55.030 --> 00:53:58.849
Were caregiver told about
the kind of symptomology

00:53:58.849 --> 00:54:01.481
of the mental illness
prior to the interview,

00:54:01.481 --> 00:54:03.685
or were they just proffered the questions?

00:54:03.685 --> 00:54:05.180
So one on the measurement of war events

00:54:05.180 --> 00:54:10.180
and the other on the kind
of context information

00:54:10.300 --> 00:54:11.630
you gave caregivers

00:54:12.567 --> 00:54:15.517
before they answered
questions on mental health.

00:54:16.584 --> 00:54:17.907
- So war events were measured

00:54:17.907 --> 00:54:21.650
with the war events
questionnaire by a collaborator,

00:54:21.650 --> 00:54:22.543
Elie Karam.

00:54:23.405 --> 00:54:26.000
He's, that has been
developed for the Middle East

00:54:26.000 --> 00:54:28.910
and has been used previously.

00:54:28.910 --> 00:54:32.920
It's basically twenty-five
items that you can sum up or,

00:54:32.920 --> 00:54:36.326
you know, that was a question
how we should use that data.

00:54:36.326 --> 00:54:37.540
So we used the total score.

00:54:37.540 --> 00:54:40.265
We've done a whole range
of analysis on that,

00:54:40.265 --> 00:54:42.953
and we'll write a paper
as well on the war events,

00:54:44.163 --> 00:54:46.013
but basically it's 25 different items

00:54:47.066 --> 00:54:50.584
for different sort of typical
war events that happen.

00:54:50.584 --> 00:54:53.683
And we ask the child about
that as well as the caregiver.

00:54:54.885 --> 00:54:56.500
We ask the child about the war exposure,

00:54:56.500 --> 00:54:58.664
the war events they've experienced,

00:54:58.664 --> 00:54:59.721
but also the caregiver

00:54:59.721 --> 00:55:00.900
about the war events
the child experienced.

00:55:00.900 --> 00:55:02.163
And we combine the two.

00:55:03.285 --> 00:55:06.580
So it's a pretty solid
measure of war events.

00:55:06.580 --> 00:55:09.481
There's still a lot of
issues with that, by the way.

00:55:09.481 --> 00:55:11.353
But I don't think I can go into that now.

00:55:12.490 --> 00:55:17.490
The mental health, so
caregivers knew a little bit

00:55:17.750 --> 00:55:20.643
about this study from
the recruitment process,

00:55:20.643 --> 00:55:21.860
but they didn't know about

00:55:21.860 --> 00:55:24.010
the specific mental health disorders

00:55:24.908 --> 00:55:26.127
when we asked about them.

00:55:26.127 --> 00:55:26.960
So we just asked the questions.

00:55:26.960 --> 00:55:29.250
They didn't have to complete
the questionnaire themselves.

00:55:29.250 --> 00:55:31.270
They were asked in interviews.

00:55:31.270 --> 00:55:32.593
So if they had questions,

00:55:33.784 --> 00:55:36.760
they could always ask the
interviewer for clarifications.

00:55:36.760 --> 00:55:40.464
And these are all established
mental health measures.

00:55:40.464 --> 00:55:41.640
And then obviously we created cutoffs

00:55:41.640 --> 00:55:43.090
based on clinical interviews.

00:55:46.640 --> 00:55:49.830
Okay, the next slide is just one example

00:55:49.830 --> 00:55:51.880
of looking at bidirectional relationships

00:55:52.743 --> 00:55:54.123
because we have longitudinal data here.

00:55:55.443 --> 00:55:57.060
And this is the example
of caregiver depression

00:55:57.060 --> 00:55:58.966
and child symptoms.

00:55:58.966 --> 00:56:02.306
So the child symptoms
here are standardized

00:56:02.306 --> 00:56:05.810
and summed across the three
mental health outcomes.

00:56:05.810 --> 00:56:09.600
So this is a continuous
measure of mental health.

00:56:09.600 --> 00:56:14.600
And importantly, we
would expect that child,

00:56:16.352 --> 00:56:17.500
that caregiver depression

00:56:17.500 --> 00:56:22.500
might increase the child
mental health symptoms, right?

00:56:22.680 --> 00:56:26.755
But what also find is
that the child symptoms,

00:56:26.755 --> 00:56:28.310
the child mental health symptoms over time

00:56:28.310 --> 00:56:31.160
has an impact on caregiver depression.

00:56:31.160 --> 00:56:33.670
And actually we find for quite a few

00:56:33.670 --> 00:56:35.163
of those risk factors.

00:56:36.252 --> 00:56:39.190
So it's not just the
environment that has an impact

00:56:39.190 --> 00:56:41.200
on the child, but it's also the child

00:56:41.200 --> 00:56:43.100
that has an impact on the environment.

00:56:44.036 --> 00:56:45.760
And I think that's a very
important aspect as well

00:56:45.760 --> 00:56:48.291
to consider for interventions.

00:56:48.291 --> 00:56:50.441
And I'll come back to
that in implications.

00:56:52.332 --> 00:56:55.033
Let me move on to the biology,

00:56:56.188 --> 00:56:59.980
and with the biology,
I'll go a bit quicker.

00:56:59.980 --> 00:57:02.472
I could say much about that,

00:57:02.472 --> 00:57:05.360
but I'll summarize those findings

00:57:06.917 --> 00:57:07.750
a little bit more succinctly.

00:57:08.717 --> 00:57:10.418
So we wanted to look here.

00:57:10.418 --> 00:57:11.480
So we still, at the beginning

00:57:11.480 --> 00:57:12.500
of looking at the biological data,

00:57:12.500 --> 00:57:15.660
COVID shutdown the labs in London,

00:57:15.660 --> 00:57:17.640
which meant that's a long time

00:57:17.640 --> 00:57:20.240
that we couldn't actually
get to our samples,

00:57:20.240 --> 00:57:22.520
but everything is analyzed now.

00:57:22.520 --> 00:57:23.723
Everything is genotyped,

00:57:24.878 --> 00:57:27.114
and everything is ready for analysis.

00:57:27.114 --> 00:57:28.530
So we've looked at the hair cortisol

00:57:28.530 --> 00:57:31.158
in relation to PTSD and war.

00:57:31.158 --> 00:57:32.340
So we wanted to know whether hair cortisol

00:57:33.179 --> 00:57:34.520
reflects exposure to war.

00:57:34.520 --> 00:57:37.243
We also wanted to look
at whether hair cortisol

00:57:37.243 --> 00:57:39.640
is associated with PTSD in children.

00:57:39.640 --> 00:57:40.560
So all of this is in children.

00:57:40.560 --> 00:57:45.076
And then we've done some work
on the role of epigenetics

00:57:45.076 --> 00:57:46.130
in psychological resilience,

00:57:46.130 --> 00:57:49.247
and that reflects work by
my postdoc Demelza Smeeth.

00:57:50.757 --> 00:57:52.875
And so first question,

00:57:52.875 --> 00:57:54.883
we looked at war exposure and cortisol,

00:57:55.999 --> 00:57:57.020
and we did find associations,

00:57:57.020 --> 00:58:00.363
but they were not very robust.

00:58:01.338 --> 00:58:03.220
When we included age of the children

00:58:04.060 --> 00:58:06.423
that correlation was
no longer significant.

00:58:08.096 --> 00:58:10.718
What we then did, we
looked at the association

00:58:10.718 --> 00:58:11.800
between war events and cortisol,

00:58:11.800 --> 00:58:16.110
depending on the age of
exposure of the children.

00:58:16.110 --> 00:58:17.890
And what we found was really interesting

00:58:17.890 --> 00:58:21.055
in that there was an association,

00:58:21.055 --> 00:58:24.410
but only in children that
were in their adolescent years

00:58:24.410 --> 00:58:26.107
during the exposure to war,

00:58:27.098 --> 00:58:32.097
suggesting that adolescence
is a sort of sensitive period

00:58:32.954 --> 00:58:37.000
or critical period for longer
lasting biological embedding

00:58:37.000 --> 00:58:38.113
of war exposure.

00:58:39.516 --> 00:58:41.340
So that was only in that group

00:58:41.340 --> 00:58:43.973
that were older than 12 years.

00:58:46.078 --> 00:58:48.000
We then looked at PTSD,

00:58:48.000 --> 00:58:51.754
and we found that there
was a robust association

00:58:51.754 --> 00:58:53.530
between PTSD and cortisol,

00:58:53.530 --> 00:58:56.202
even when controlling
for all the covariates.

00:58:56.202 --> 00:58:58.320
And we have a lot of covariates,

00:58:58.320 --> 00:58:59.870
and we control for all of them.

00:59:01.678 --> 00:59:04.363
In the next step we looked
at the mediating effects.

00:59:05.473 --> 00:59:07.630
We wanted to see whether war exposure,

00:59:07.630 --> 00:59:11.400
whether cortisol or PTSD mediates

00:59:11.400 --> 00:59:13.630
the effects of war exposure.

00:59:13.630 --> 00:59:16.899
And what we found is that
there is a partial mediation

00:59:16.899 --> 00:59:21.323
between of the effect of war
exposure and cortisol by PTSD.

00:59:22.408 --> 00:59:26.284
When we ran the opposite testing,

00:59:26.284 --> 00:59:27.700
where the cortisol was mediating effects

00:59:27.700 --> 00:59:29.980
between war and PTSD,

00:59:29.980 --> 00:59:34.103
there was also a partial
mediation, but it was a lot less.

00:59:34.103 --> 00:59:35.110
So it seems the case

00:59:35.110 --> 00:59:40.110
that war leads to elevated hair cortisol,

00:59:42.100 --> 00:59:44.690
particularly in those
children that develop PTSD

00:59:44.690 --> 00:59:47.173
in relation to the war exposure.

00:59:50.560 --> 00:59:52.140
And I'll leave it at that.

00:59:52.140 --> 00:59:55.483
I know that's not very
much, but basically,

00:59:57.642 --> 00:59:59.040
so the distance between the war exposure

00:59:59.040 --> 01:00:04.040
and the measurement of cortisol
is between two to four years

01:00:04.266 --> 01:00:05.860
in our sample.

01:00:05.860 --> 01:00:08.225
Right, so that's quite a long distance.

01:00:08.225 --> 01:00:09.720
So that's quite a long distance
from the initial exposure.

01:00:09.720 --> 01:00:11.750
And that was particularly the case

01:00:11.750 --> 01:00:14.970
in children that were in
their adolescent years

01:00:14.970 --> 01:00:15.803
during their war exposure.

01:00:16.650 --> 01:00:21.061
And I just want to mention
for the role of epigenetics,

01:00:21.061 --> 01:00:24.320
we actually haven't analyzed the data yet,

01:00:24.320 --> 01:00:25.363
but what we've done,

01:00:26.226 --> 01:00:30.430
we've developed the theoretical
framework that we will use

01:00:30.430 --> 01:00:33.110
to look at epigenetics,

01:00:33.110 --> 01:00:37.528
and to take into account genetic factors

01:00:37.528 --> 01:00:40.744
because genetic factors
act upon the epigenome

01:00:40.744 --> 01:00:42.422
or DNA methylation,

01:00:42.422 --> 01:00:45.990
but they might also be
stable methylation over time,

01:00:45.990 --> 01:00:49.170
and then obviously there
are protective factors

01:00:49.170 --> 01:00:52.710
and adversity that's together

01:00:52.710 --> 01:00:57.030
then results in epigenetic signatures

01:00:57.030 --> 01:00:59.013
that are associated with resilience.

01:01:00.460 --> 01:01:03.922
There's a lot more to be
said about that, but I don't,

01:01:03.922 --> 01:01:06.122
we don't have any analysis yet,

01:01:06.122 --> 01:01:08.984
but this is basically the
framework that we're gonna use.

01:01:08.984 --> 01:01:12.618
I'm happy to share that paper
with anyone that's interested

01:01:12.618 --> 01:01:13.773
in having a closer look at that,

01:01:14.830 --> 01:01:17.300
but let me move on to the treatment

01:01:17.300 --> 01:01:18.790
of Syrian refugee children

01:01:19.690 --> 01:01:22.623
and how to do that over telephone.

01:01:24.141 --> 01:01:26.353
We thought that was a really
innovative thing to do,

01:01:27.220 --> 01:01:29.350
and then COVID-19 hits, and
suddenly the whole world

01:01:29.350 --> 01:01:31.633
was doing treatment over the telephone.

01:01:32.744 --> 01:01:35.902
But actually when I was
thinking about the idea,

01:01:35.902 --> 01:01:39.100
and I spoke to several people
in the field before COVID,

01:01:39.100 --> 01:01:40.620
most people said, no,
we don't really do that

01:01:40.620 --> 01:01:43.350
in those settings, and
no one was, you know,

01:01:43.350 --> 01:01:45.600
even thinking about
doing that in the future.

01:01:46.580 --> 01:01:49.220
But why could it be important?

01:01:49.220 --> 01:01:52.763
Well, we know that children
that experience war

01:01:52.763 --> 01:01:54.481
are at the higher risk for development

01:01:54.481 --> 01:01:55.604
of mental health problems,

01:01:55.604 --> 01:01:58.483
but also that many
don't receive treatment.

01:01:58.483 --> 01:02:00.184
And that's for a range of reasons.

01:02:00.184 --> 01:02:04.730
It's often very difficult
to set up new services

01:02:04.730 --> 01:02:05.853
in those settings.

01:02:07.005 --> 01:02:09.623
It's difficult to recruit qualified staff,

01:02:09.623 --> 01:02:11.881
and refugees are often not mobile.

01:02:11.881 --> 01:02:13.780
They can't just go to
a clinic that easily.

01:02:13.780 --> 01:02:18.200
So we wanted to adapt
an existing treatment

01:02:18.200 --> 01:02:19.823
for delivery over telephone,

01:02:20.880 --> 01:02:23.950
with the help of trained and
supervised lay counselors.

01:02:23.950 --> 01:02:25.340
And then we wanted to evaluate

01:02:25.340 --> 01:02:27.510
the feasibility,
acceptability and efficacy

01:02:27.510 --> 01:02:30.320
of this new treatment with
Syrian refugee children

01:02:30.320 --> 01:02:31.804
in Lebanon.

01:02:31.804 --> 01:02:33.963
And we were using that, we basically,

01:02:33.963 --> 01:02:38.963
using the BIOPATH study to recruit people

01:02:39.240 --> 01:02:41.110
into our clinical trial.

01:02:41.110 --> 01:02:43.333
And basically these are all the children,

01:02:44.203 --> 01:02:47.001
all the families that
said they would like help,

01:02:47.001 --> 01:02:48.960
if they met inclusion criteria
for our clinical trial,

01:02:48.960 --> 01:02:52.218
they were offered inclusion
in the clinical trial.

01:02:52.218 --> 01:02:54.596
If not, they were
receiving other treatments.

01:02:54.596 --> 01:02:56.080
We had several psychologists working

01:02:56.080 --> 01:02:57.970
and counselors working with us.

01:02:57.970 --> 01:03:00.773
So we provided free treatment
to every single person

01:03:00.773 --> 01:03:03.960
that was associated with,
affiliated with the family

01:03:03.960 --> 01:03:05.560
that participated in this study.

01:03:08.938 --> 01:03:11.359
So this is basically the BIOPATH sample.

01:03:11.359 --> 01:03:12.775
The intervention is CETA,

01:03:12.775 --> 01:03:15.115
Common Elements Treatment Approach.

01:03:15.115 --> 01:03:17.710
And this is a modular,
transdiagnostic therapy

01:03:18.556 --> 01:03:20.273
developed for those kinds of settings.

01:03:21.541 --> 01:03:23.195
And it's based on the most effective

01:03:23.195 --> 01:03:24.873
cognitive behavioral therapy components.

01:03:25.819 --> 01:03:27.839
We first developed the t-CETA manual

01:03:27.839 --> 01:03:30.900
by delivering CETA
face-to-face and some piloting,

01:03:30.900 --> 01:03:34.010
and then we run a
randomized control trial,

01:03:34.010 --> 01:03:35.560
which ended up being very small

01:03:36.762 --> 01:03:39.163
to evaluate the feasibility,
acceptability and efficacy.

01:03:40.100 --> 01:03:40.940
The measures that we used

01:03:41.800 --> 01:03:45.620
were the same mental health
symptoms scales that we used

01:03:45.620 --> 01:03:48.770
for the rest of the BIOPATH study.

01:03:48.770 --> 01:03:51.213
We used the same clinical interview.

01:03:52.160 --> 01:03:54.563
In addition, we have
in session assessments,

01:03:55.455 --> 01:03:56.934
that was the client monitoring form,

01:03:56.934 --> 01:03:58.119
which is part of the treatment.

01:03:58.119 --> 01:04:00.740
So every session the
counselor is completing

01:04:00.740 --> 01:04:02.963
a short assessment of the child.

01:04:04.070 --> 01:04:07.270
Also, the PSYCHLOPS was
used at the beginning

01:04:07.270 --> 01:04:09.470
and the last session,
as well in the middle.

01:04:10.769 --> 01:04:14.533
And then we also completed
some qualitative research.

01:04:14.533 --> 01:04:17.641
So we interviewed the counselors
that delivered t-CETA,

01:04:17.641 --> 01:04:20.822
and we interviewed 11
families that received t-CETA,

01:04:20.822 --> 01:04:22.523
so both the child as
well as the caregiver.

01:04:23.580 --> 01:04:28.223
We ended up with a very
small sample in RCT.

01:04:29.140 --> 01:04:32.118
It was very difficult
recruiting children and families

01:04:32.118 --> 01:04:32.951
into this study.

01:04:33.920 --> 01:04:38.090
Actually from all the
children that initially,

01:04:38.090 --> 01:04:41.320
and the families that initially
expressed an interest,

01:04:41.320 --> 01:04:44.779
only 7% ended up receiving treatment,

01:04:44.779 --> 01:04:46.879
even though that treatment was over phone.

01:04:47.740 --> 01:04:50.375
Because we ended up
with a very small sample

01:04:50.375 --> 01:04:53.460
of about 20 children,
so 10 in the treatment

01:04:53.460 --> 01:04:55.153
and 10 in the control condition,

01:04:56.017 --> 01:04:57.720
the control condition
was treatment as usual.

01:04:57.720 --> 01:05:01.850
We used the patient analysis
approach to help us with that.

01:05:01.850 --> 01:05:04.050
And we were able to show that

01:05:04.050 --> 01:05:09.050
there are significant treatment effects

01:05:09.640 --> 01:05:13.010
of the t-CETA condition
with a moderate effect size

01:05:14.359 --> 01:05:15.192
of 0.33.

01:05:16.074 --> 01:05:16.907
- Sorry to interrupt.

01:05:16.907 --> 01:05:18.657
Do you mind saying a little bit

01:05:18.657 --> 01:05:21.281
about why you think
the take-up was so low?

01:05:21.281 --> 01:05:24.434
I mean, it seems this is a
very vulnerable population.

01:05:24.434 --> 01:05:27.039
Are there security concerns?

01:05:27.039 --> 01:05:30.557
They don't agree with intervention.

01:05:30.557 --> 01:05:32.659
Yeah, it would be great to just--

01:05:32.659 --> 01:05:34.870
- Yeah, so we actually have
conducted a qualitative study

01:05:34.870 --> 01:05:35.913
exactly on that.

01:05:37.099 --> 01:05:42.099
And to some degree when they were asked

01:05:43.400 --> 01:05:48.040
to attend the intake session
which was in the clinic,

01:05:48.040 --> 01:05:50.003
the intake session was in the clinic.

01:05:50.840 --> 01:05:53.636
The treatment was over the
phone or in the clinic,

01:05:53.636 --> 01:05:55.503
depending on what they
were randomized into.

01:05:56.410 --> 01:05:58.600
Quite a few parents thought
they no longer have,

01:05:58.600 --> 01:06:00.470
the children no longer have a problem,

01:06:00.470 --> 01:06:02.163
or it's actually not too bad.

01:06:03.820 --> 01:06:05.155
I think there were work commitments

01:06:05.155 --> 01:06:06.605
that were getting in the way.

01:06:07.560 --> 01:06:10.640
Work commitments of the
child or the caregiver,

01:06:10.640 --> 01:06:12.650
transport issues.

01:06:12.650 --> 01:06:14.513
So quite a few logistic problems,

01:06:15.692 --> 01:06:20.692
but also I would say a
limited understanding

01:06:20.730 --> 01:06:23.493
of mental health problems
that need treatment.

01:06:24.665 --> 01:06:26.223
So these were some of
the points that came up,

01:06:27.719 --> 01:06:30.519
but we we're still working
on the analysis of that data.

01:06:31.956 --> 01:06:32.960
- And Michael,

01:06:32.960 --> 01:06:34.940
that's a really interesting
observation actually,

01:06:34.940 --> 01:06:38.237
but I'm just flagging that we
have seven minutes left, so.

01:06:38.237 --> 01:06:41.013
- Yes, yes I will, I will get through.

01:06:42.417 --> 01:06:45.720
So the positive effects of the treatment

01:06:46.913 --> 01:06:49.940
was mostly driven by the
reduction in depression symptoms.

01:06:49.940 --> 01:06:53.319
Then we also looked at
the in-session assessment,

01:06:53.319 --> 01:06:56.198
and you can see here with
the client monitoring form

01:06:56.198 --> 01:06:59.660
that there is really a very
steep decrease over time

01:07:00.755 --> 01:07:02.643
in all the children
that receive treatment.

01:07:03.576 --> 01:07:05.277
That was also shown in the PSYCHLOPS,

01:07:05.277 --> 01:07:07.070
which measures problems,
functioning, well-being.

01:07:07.070 --> 01:07:10.660
So it was hard baseline and
then significantly reduced

01:07:11.877 --> 01:07:13.758
towards the final session,

01:07:13.758 --> 01:07:15.023
and these changes were significant.

01:07:16.470 --> 01:07:21.053
The qualitative findings, I
could say a lot about that,

01:07:21.053 --> 01:07:22.373
but I'll keep that really short.

01:07:22.373 --> 01:07:23.700
So we looked at the feasibility

01:07:23.700 --> 01:07:25.343
by interviewing the counselors.

01:07:26.599 --> 01:07:28.314
Three themes emerged.

01:07:28.314 --> 01:07:32.520
Basically they found that
delivering therapy over the phone

01:07:32.520 --> 01:07:34.314
solves some problems,

01:07:34.314 --> 01:07:36.276
but it also creates some
additional practical

01:07:36.276 --> 01:07:37.910
and logistical challenges

01:07:37.910 --> 01:07:40.270
such as bad connections with the phones

01:07:40.270 --> 01:07:42.950
or finding a space where
the child could speak

01:07:43.799 --> 01:07:45.013
without being heard by others.

01:07:45.935 --> 01:07:49.220
They generally found that
the t-CETA was adapted well

01:07:50.057 --> 01:07:53.975
to overcome cultural blocks
and that t-CETA works

01:07:53.975 --> 01:07:55.957
and is needed.

01:07:55.957 --> 01:07:59.580
To some degree, they were
surprised that it worked

01:07:59.580 --> 01:08:00.733
as well as it did.

01:08:01.940 --> 01:08:05.150
I think some of the counselors
were quite skeptical

01:08:05.150 --> 01:08:06.350
whether this would work,

01:08:07.532 --> 01:08:09.213
but generally they found
that it would work.

01:08:10.154 --> 01:08:13.445
When we interviewed the
caregivers and the children,

01:08:13.445 --> 01:08:16.063
four themes emerged,
actually quite similar.

01:08:16.063 --> 01:08:17.738
Again, that it solves some problems,

01:08:17.738 --> 01:08:19.960
but also creates some challenges,

01:08:19.960 --> 01:08:22.460
that t-CETA works for most families,

01:08:22.460 --> 01:08:24.820
but there are some challenges that remain

01:08:24.820 --> 01:08:27.200
in other families that
can't be solved easily

01:08:28.058 --> 01:08:30.381
because the challenging context

01:08:30.381 --> 01:08:32.250
remains a challenging context.

01:08:32.250 --> 01:08:33.960
Another factor was that the relationship

01:08:33.960 --> 01:08:37.100
between the counselor and
the child and caregiver

01:08:37.100 --> 01:08:37.933
was very important

01:08:39.521 --> 01:08:40.420
and that a family's
attitude to mental health

01:08:40.420 --> 01:08:42.483
was also very important.

01:08:43.318 --> 01:08:45.143
Important to mention,

01:08:46.098 --> 01:08:47.610
this intervention was not just focusing

01:08:47.610 --> 01:08:49.599
on the individual child,

01:08:49.599 --> 01:08:52.180
but there was also a
parenting component to it.

01:08:52.180 --> 01:08:53.963
So caregivers were involved in it.

01:08:55.210 --> 01:09:00.210
So let me, in the last few
minutes, discuss what we found.

01:09:01.396 --> 01:09:03.995
So basically we found mental
health problems are frequent.

01:09:03.995 --> 01:09:06.395
Every second child reports
significant problems.

01:09:07.410 --> 01:09:12.357
25.6% of caregivers when asked

01:09:12.357 --> 01:09:15.257
said they would like to receive
treatment for their child.

01:09:15.257 --> 01:09:18.617
So that suggests there is a
significant perceived need

01:09:18.617 --> 01:09:21.720
for mental health services and
also willingness to use them.

01:09:21.720 --> 01:09:23.010
However, the majority of these children

01:09:23.010 --> 01:09:25.195
never received treatment.

01:09:25.195 --> 01:09:27.383
And that is to various barriers.

01:09:27.383 --> 01:09:30.270
We found that about 20% of the sample

01:09:30.270 --> 01:09:32.600
met our criteria for resilience.

01:09:32.600 --> 01:09:35.197
That is a lot less than in other studies,

01:09:35.197 --> 01:09:36.799
and the reason it's a lot less

01:09:36.799 --> 01:09:38.535
is because we defined resilience

01:09:38.535 --> 01:09:41.437
as being below the cutoffs
of multiple dimensions

01:09:41.437 --> 01:09:42.310
of mental health outcomes.

01:09:42.310 --> 01:09:44.890
If we would have looked at only PTSD

01:09:44.890 --> 01:09:47.972
and considered anyone
that is below the cutoff

01:09:47.972 --> 01:09:48.805
for PTSD for resilience,

01:09:48.805 --> 01:09:52.040
our resilience proportion
would be much more similar

01:09:52.040 --> 01:09:54.097
to previous studies,

01:09:54.097 --> 01:09:55.996
but it's really important to look at it

01:09:55.996 --> 01:09:57.346
across multiple dimensions.

01:09:58.997 --> 01:10:00.060
And we identified a range of factors

01:10:00.060 --> 01:10:02.310
that are associated with
risk and resilience.

01:10:04.518 --> 01:10:07.090
We found that war exposure
and mental health problems

01:10:07.090 --> 01:10:09.710
are associated with biological factors

01:10:09.710 --> 01:10:12.870
and that biological embedding
seems to be particularly

01:10:12.870 --> 01:10:16.470
in those children the
case that were exposed to

01:10:17.515 --> 01:10:18.350
in adolescence.

01:10:18.350 --> 01:10:20.770
We successfully adapted a treatment

01:10:20.770 --> 01:10:22.453
to be delivered over telephone.

01:10:23.940 --> 01:10:27.116
Children allocated to t-CETA,

01:10:27.116 --> 01:10:29.012
so telephone-delivered CETA

01:10:29.012 --> 01:10:31.000
significantly improved over time,

01:10:31.000 --> 01:10:33.140
but the randomized controlled
trial was very small,

01:10:33.140 --> 01:10:37.317
and findings need to be
replicated in larger studies.

01:10:37.317 --> 01:10:39.410
But the qualitative research suggests

01:10:39.410 --> 01:10:43.140
that both counselors and
families that received help

01:10:43.140 --> 01:10:44.893
found the treatment effective.

01:10:46.581 --> 01:10:48.250
But t-CETA may not be
a solution that works

01:10:48.250 --> 01:10:50.773
in every situation and every family.

01:10:52.055 --> 01:10:53.976
And I want to end with
some of the implications.

01:10:53.976 --> 01:10:56.630
The main implications of this
is that many refugee children

01:10:56.630 --> 01:10:59.259
suffer from significant
mental health problems.

01:10:59.259 --> 01:11:02.500
We know that, but that number
is actually quite high.

01:11:02.500 --> 01:11:05.818
And most of these children
never receive treatment.

01:11:05.818 --> 01:11:07.810
And even when we offer them
treatment over the phone,

01:11:07.810 --> 01:11:10.613
most of them don't receive treatment,

01:11:10.613 --> 01:11:13.638
and actually quite a few
children that receive treatment

01:11:13.638 --> 01:11:14.776
drop out of treatment.

01:11:14.776 --> 01:11:16.840
We have a lot less dropout
in the phone condition,

01:11:16.840 --> 01:11:21.840
but with traditional services,
many children will drop out.

01:11:24.197 --> 01:11:26.550
We've identified several
predictors that point to the family

01:11:26.550 --> 01:11:30.120
and the wider social context
as particularly relevant.

01:11:30.120 --> 01:11:32.180
And this means interventions should focus

01:11:32.180 --> 01:11:36.038
not just on the individual,
but also the social context.

01:11:36.038 --> 01:11:37.720
So systemic approaches
are particularly important

01:11:37.720 --> 01:11:41.110
as well as the mental
health of the caregiver

01:11:41.978 --> 01:11:42.999
is very important.

01:11:42.999 --> 01:11:45.176
We need to treat the
caregiver's mental health

01:11:45.176 --> 01:11:46.570
in order to help the child.

01:11:46.570 --> 01:11:49.420
So standard delivery of
treatment and programs

01:11:49.420 --> 01:11:52.130
that are usually delivered in clinics

01:11:52.130 --> 01:11:54.770
is unlikely to reach children in need.

01:11:54.770 --> 01:11:58.755
Treatment over phone will
overcome some of those barriers,

01:11:58.755 --> 01:12:00.501
but not, will be not sufficient.

01:12:00.501 --> 01:12:02.250
Will not be sufficient.

01:12:02.250 --> 01:12:05.290
I think what is really needed
is community-based approaches

01:12:05.290 --> 01:12:07.130
that involve community members

01:12:07.130 --> 01:12:09.190
and that reach not only the child

01:12:09.190 --> 01:12:11.890
but also the social
context, the caregivers,

01:12:11.890 --> 01:12:14.970
that will be absolutely
crucial to reach more children

01:12:14.970 --> 01:12:16.983
and to overcome this treatment gap.

01:12:18.140 --> 01:12:20.930
Just an acknowledgement
of my research partners

01:12:20.930 --> 01:12:23.320
of the various funding that we received.

01:12:23.320 --> 01:12:25.703
And I thank you very
much for your attention.

01:12:27.980 --> 01:12:29.800
- Thank you very much, Michael.

01:12:29.800 --> 01:12:31.710
Thank you very much for threading

01:12:32.998 --> 01:12:35.550
all these disciplines
through: epigenetics,

01:12:35.550 --> 01:12:39.150
psychology, community-based programming.

01:12:39.150 --> 01:12:41.177
Thank you very much indeed.

01:12:41.177 --> 01:12:43.882
When I see your last slide,

01:12:43.882 --> 01:12:45.892
I see you need an
anthropologist on your team,

01:12:45.892 --> 01:12:47.210
but most importantly, you know,

01:12:47.210 --> 01:12:50.710
maybe you could just say a few
words on your local partners

01:12:50.710 --> 01:12:54.370
because community-based
approaches are spearheaded

01:12:54.370 --> 01:12:56.003
by local partners.

01:12:56.938 --> 01:12:58.730
Could you just tell us
a little bit about that?

01:12:58.730 --> 01:13:00.650
And then if there are other questions,

01:13:00.650 --> 01:13:02.780
please raise your hand
and put them in the chat.

01:13:02.780 --> 01:13:04.620
I know there was just a detailed question

01:13:04.620 --> 01:13:06.930
on the CETA approach,

01:13:06.930 --> 01:13:09.900
how many sessions and over
what time was that provided

01:13:09.900 --> 01:13:12.443
to people to accept.

01:13:12.443 --> 01:13:15.241
But I'd really like to
ask you a quick question

01:13:15.241 --> 01:13:18.390
on how local partners took this together.

01:13:18.390 --> 01:13:21.180
Not just the people who were
implementing in the field,

01:13:21.180 --> 01:13:23.060
but how they co-designed perhaps,

01:13:23.060 --> 01:13:28.060
or co-implemented or see the
future directions of your work.

01:13:30.320 --> 01:13:32.330
- Yeah, so this is, this
is future directions.

01:13:32.330 --> 01:13:37.330
We are working on developing
a community-based approach

01:13:37.970 --> 01:13:41.670
based on our experience in Lebanon.

01:13:41.670 --> 01:13:45.290
And we, in order to
increase the recruitment

01:13:45.290 --> 01:13:47.960
into the randomized control trial,

01:13:47.960 --> 01:13:50.830
we've been running sessions
in the settlements,

01:13:50.830 --> 01:13:52.963
and those have been very helpful.

01:13:54.332 --> 01:13:57.160
And that's really sort of where we felt

01:13:57.160 --> 01:14:00.960
that we really have to do
something in the settlements,

01:14:00.960 --> 01:14:04.743
because for them to leave
for refugee families,

01:14:05.669 --> 01:14:08.728
especially many, many of
those have many children.

01:14:08.728 --> 01:14:09.730
So if one child has a problem,

01:14:09.730 --> 01:14:12.326
what do they do with
all the other children?

01:14:12.326 --> 01:14:13.270
That's an issue.

01:14:13.270 --> 01:14:15.993
And so that makes them much more,

01:14:17.230 --> 01:14:21.587
much less likely to
actually seek services,

01:14:21.587 --> 01:14:23.780
even if they're provided not
too far from the settlement,

01:14:23.780 --> 01:14:25.860
because it's difficult to get there.

01:14:25.860 --> 01:14:30.860
So yeah, delivering services
right in the settlements

01:14:31.770 --> 01:14:34.270
is what they told us what they would like.

01:14:34.270 --> 01:14:37.627
But also there is some stigma issues.

01:14:37.627 --> 01:14:39.652
They wouldn't like that other people know

01:14:39.652 --> 01:14:41.770
that they received treatments.

01:14:41.770 --> 01:14:45.680
That was more problem, more a
problem in some settlements,

01:14:45.680 --> 01:14:46.850
less in other settlements.

01:14:46.850 --> 01:14:50.403
So there was quite a bit of
variability between settlements,

01:14:51.508 --> 01:14:53.910
but we found that they
actually were very engaged.

01:14:53.910 --> 01:14:57.500
So we went, I was, we
designed the sessions.

01:14:57.500 --> 01:15:01.604
We went with local staff
into those settlements

01:15:01.604 --> 01:15:04.483
and delivered those sessions.

01:15:04.483 --> 01:15:05.316
Well, I didn't deliver them,

01:15:05.316 --> 01:15:08.830
but our field stuff
delivered them in Arabic.

01:15:08.830 --> 01:15:13.700
And we had large groups
joining in these discussions

01:15:13.700 --> 01:15:15.313
and very lively discussions.

01:15:16.602 --> 01:15:19.160
And it was actually quite
helpful to identify families

01:15:19.160 --> 01:15:21.910
that really are worried about
their child because they,

01:15:22.767 --> 01:15:24.364
there is significant problem.

01:15:24.364 --> 01:15:26.726
Whereas there were quite a
few that thought, you know,

01:15:26.726 --> 01:15:29.449
the treatment may be,
you know, interesting,

01:15:29.449 --> 01:15:32.829
but they don't necessarily,
didn't see a clear need.

01:15:32.829 --> 01:15:37.829
I think big part of that
would be psycho education,

01:15:37.848 --> 01:15:42.220
to inform them about the
problems that we typically see

01:15:42.220 --> 01:15:46.009
in children and at what
level do they need treatment.

01:15:46.009 --> 01:15:48.630
But also I think an
important aspect would be

01:15:48.630 --> 01:15:52.180
to maybe deliver some
sessions on resilience

01:15:53.234 --> 01:15:54.792
and what promotes resilience

01:15:54.792 --> 01:15:57.543
and families, strengthening
families and parenting.

01:15:58.831 --> 01:16:00.300
So that's what I would do.

01:16:00.300 --> 01:16:02.040
What I think would be very crucial

01:16:02.040 --> 01:16:06.650
is to recruit community
members from those settlements

01:16:06.650 --> 01:16:09.800
to actually deliver some
of that or co-deliver that

01:16:10.814 --> 01:16:11.647
and co-create it.

01:16:13.099 --> 01:16:14.260
I think that's absolutely key.

01:16:14.260 --> 01:16:15.796
- Thank you.

01:16:15.796 --> 01:16:19.440
Are there any quick questions?

01:16:19.440 --> 01:16:22.710
Most of our participants say
thank you for a great talk,

01:16:22.710 --> 01:16:27.710
but have to go at this cutoff
point before nightfall.

01:16:29.240 --> 01:16:30.073
This is winter.

01:16:31.230 --> 01:16:34.380
I'm just checking, but mostly
thank you very much indeed.

01:16:34.380 --> 01:16:38.070
Michael, that was a
really great presentation.

01:16:38.070 --> 01:16:39.850
I'm actually going to ask
you one more question.

01:16:39.850 --> 01:16:42.420
Sorry, I'm really interested in this

01:16:42.420 --> 01:16:43.983
because in the Middle Eastern context,

01:16:43.983 --> 01:16:45.780
gender is a big dimension.

01:16:45.780 --> 01:16:49.440
So we know that PTSD is
different across gender.

01:16:49.440 --> 01:16:53.736
And maybe that, I know
you probably treated that

01:16:53.736 --> 01:16:55.786
as a covariate rather than anything else,

01:16:56.660 --> 01:16:59.730
but do you have any last
thoughts on the gender dimension

01:16:59.730 --> 01:17:00.563
of your work.

01:17:01.617 --> 01:17:04.020
Interestingly the caregivers
were mothers, right?

01:17:04.020 --> 01:17:05.100
Do you have any?

01:17:05.100 --> 01:17:07.750
- Yes, so we actually, so we
looked at the prevalence rates

01:17:07.750 --> 01:17:11.020
separately between boys and girls,

01:17:11.020 --> 01:17:13.200
and externalizing problems,
as you can imagine,

01:17:13.200 --> 01:17:15.120
are more prevalent amongst boys,

01:17:15.120 --> 01:17:17.900
and depression more amongst girls.

01:17:17.900 --> 01:17:19.350
So there are some differences

01:17:20.338 --> 01:17:25.170
I don't remember regarding war exposure,

01:17:25.170 --> 01:17:27.158
whether there was some difference.

01:17:27.158 --> 01:17:29.830
I could imagine that
the boys are more likely

01:17:29.830 --> 01:17:30.850
to experience war exposure

01:17:30.850 --> 01:17:34.497
because they're more likely
to be outside than the girls.

01:17:34.497 --> 01:17:37.520
I think that's also the
case in the settlements.

01:17:37.520 --> 01:17:39.916
Some of the families that I visited,

01:17:39.916 --> 01:17:44.916
it seemed that the children
are mostly within the tent

01:17:45.050 --> 01:17:47.210
or within the contain they were living in.

01:17:47.210 --> 01:17:48.510
Whereas in other families,

01:17:49.502 --> 01:17:51.260
the children were just
basically outside all the time

01:17:51.260 --> 01:17:52.313
by themselves.

01:17:53.310 --> 01:17:57.473
So it could vary quite a bit
between different families.

01:17:59.255 --> 01:18:00.254
Yeah.

01:18:00.254 --> 01:18:03.458
- Anyway, thank you very
much for that big picture,

01:18:03.458 --> 01:18:08.458
for the very careful detailed
analysis of people in need.

01:18:09.036 --> 01:18:12.757
And it was a pleasure
to have you talk to us

01:18:12.757 --> 01:18:14.192
at Yale, and thank you very much.

01:18:14.192 --> 01:18:15.259
- You're welcome.

01:18:15.259 --> 01:18:16.258
Thanks for inviting me.

01:18:16.258 --> 01:18:18.614
And thanks for the patience.

01:18:18.614 --> 01:18:21.560
I realize there's a lot of findings,

01:18:21.560 --> 01:18:23.403
but I'm excited about all our findings.

01:18:23.403 --> 01:18:25.710
I sort of wanted to share
what we have at this stage,

01:18:25.710 --> 01:18:26.810
and there's obviously,

01:18:27.870 --> 01:18:30.160
there's lots more to
be done with the data.

01:18:30.160 --> 01:18:33.700
And if anyone is
interested to collaborate,

01:18:33.700 --> 01:18:35.855
please get in touch with me.

01:18:35.855 --> 01:18:37.974
Anyone, that might be possibility.

01:18:37.974 --> 01:18:39.000
- Well, yours is a landmark study,

01:18:39.000 --> 01:18:40.900
so thank you very much indeed.

01:18:40.900 --> 01:18:41.890
Take good care.

01:18:41.890 --> 01:18:42.740
- You're welcome.

01:18:43.634 --> 01:18:44.467
Take care!

